window.CURRICULUM = [{"id":"start","title":"Start here: what DevOps is","level":"Beginner","icon":"🚀","summary":"What DevOps means, what a DevOps engineer does, and the project you'll be able to build by the end.","topic":"DevOps","lessons":[{"title":"What DevOps is","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The problem DevOps solves","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Software used to be built by one team (<strong>Dev</strong>) and run by another (<strong>Ops</strong>). Developers wanted to ship changes fast. Operations wanted nothing to break. Code was \"thrown over the wall\", releases were rare and scary, and when something failed each side blamed the other.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  D[Developers<br/>want change] -- \"throw code over the wall\" --> W{{Release day 😬}}\n  W --> O[Operations<br/>want stability]\n  O -. \"it broke, your fault\" .-> D","caption":"The old way: a wall between the teams"}]},{"type":"card","title":"So what is DevOps?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>DevOps</strong> is a way of working where the people who build software and the people who run it share responsibility for the whole life of a service: plan, code, build, test, release, run and learn.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>culture plus automation</strong>. Tools such as Docker, Terraform and GitHub Actions matter, but only because they let small changes reach users <strong>quickly and safely</strong>, again and again.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The DevOps loop","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Work flows round a never-ending loop. Each lap should be small, so each release is low-risk and you learn quickly from real users.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  P[Plan] --> C[Code] --> B[Build] --> T[Test]\n  T --> R[Release] --> D[Deploy] --> O[Operate] --> M[Monitor]\n  M --> P","caption":"Plan → code → build → test → release → deploy → operate → monitor → back to plan"}]},{"type":"card","title":"CALMS: the five ideas behind DevOps","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ulture: shared ownership, and no blame when things go wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>utomation: anything you do twice by hand, script it.</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>ean: small batches, little waiting, little waste.</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>easurement: decide with data (deploy frequency, failure rate, recovery time).</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>haring: knowledge, tools and on-call duty are shared, not hoarded.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Measuring DevOps: the four DORA metrics","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Google's DORA research found four numbers that separate strong teams from weak ones:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Deployment frequency</td>\n<td>How often do we ship?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lead time for changes</td>\n<td>How long from commit to production?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change failure rate</td>\n<td>What % of releases cause a problem?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time to restore</td>\n<td>How fast do we recover?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Elite teams deploy often <strong>and</strong> fail rarely. Speed and stability go together.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the main goal of DevOps?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To replace operations engineers with developers","ok":false},{"t":"To get small changes to users quickly and safely, with shared ownership","ok":true},{"t":"To use as many tools as possible","ok":false},{"t":"To release software less often so it is more stable","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>DevOps is culture and automation that make <strong>frequent, low-risk</strong> releases possible. Releasing <em>less</em> often usually makes each release <em>riskier</em>.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m167--type-html-html-what-is-the-ma"}}]},{"title":"What a DevOps engineer does","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"A day in the job","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A DevOps or platform engineer builds the <strong>road</strong> that code travels on, so developers can ship without tickets and waiting:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>writes <strong>infrastructure as code</strong> (Terraform) for networks, servers and databases</li>\n<li>builds <strong>CI/CD pipelines</strong> that test and deploy automatically</li>\n<li>packages apps in <strong>containers</strong></li>\n<li>sets up <strong>monitoring, logging and alerts</strong></li>\n<li>keeps things <strong>secure</strong>, <strong>reliable</strong> and <strong>affordable</strong></li>\n<li>helps fix <strong>incidents</strong> and makes sure they don't repeat</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The skills stack","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You learn these roughly from the bottom up. This app follows the same order.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart BT\n  L[Linux + the terminal] --> N[Networking]\n  N --> G[Git]\n  G --> D[Docker]\n  D --> A[Cloud: AWS]\n  A --> T[Terraform]\n  T --> C[CI/CD]\n  C --> O[Monitoring + security]\n  O --> K[Kubernetes + scale]","caption":"Each layer builds on the one below"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Tip:</strong> you don't need to master one layer before starting the next. You do need the basics of each, because every layer leans on the ones below.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these is NOT usually part of a DevOps engineer's job?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Writing Terraform for cloud infrastructure","ok":false},{"t":"Building CI/CD pipelines","ok":false},{"t":"Designing the company logo","ok":true},{"t":"Setting up monitoring and alerts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Branding is design work. Infrastructure as code, pipelines and monitoring are core DevOps tasks.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m168--type-html-html-which-of-these"}}]},{"title":"The project you'll build","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Meet Memos","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Memos</strong> is an open-source note-taking web app (Go backend, React frontend). In this course you take its code and run it on AWS the way a real company would, live at <strong>memos.example.com</strong>.</p>\n<p>By the end you'll understand every box in this picture, and be able to build it from an empty AWS account. The <strong>Build Lab</strong> tab walks you through it step by step.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The finished architecture","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  U([👤 User]) -->|HTTPS| R53[Route 53<br/>DNS]\n  R53 --> ALB\n  subgraph VPC[\"VPC 10.0.0.0/16 · eu-west-1\"]\n    subgraph PUB[Public subnets · 2 AZs]\n      ALB[Application Load Balancer<br/>TLS with ACM cert]\n      NAT[NAT gateways]\n    end\n    subgraph PRIV[Private subnets · 2 AZs]\n      ECS[ECS Fargate task<br/>Memos on :8081]\n    end\n  end\n  ALB -->|:8081| ECS\n  ECS -->|pull image| ECR[(ECR)]\n  ECS -->|logs| CW[CloudWatch]\n  ECS -. outbound .-> NAT","caption":"What a request to memos.example.com passes through"}]},{"type":"card","title":"How code gets there","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Nobody clicks around the AWS console. A push to GitHub starts a pipeline that builds, scans and deploys, and logs in to AWS <strong>without any stored keys</strong> (OIDC).</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  Dev[💻 git push] --> GH[GitHub]\n  GH --> B[build.yml<br/>tfsec · docker build]\n  B -->|push image :sha| ECR[(ECR)]\n  B --> D[deploy.yml<br/>terraform plan/apply]\n  D --> ECS[ECS service<br/>rolls to new image]\n  ECS --> H{curl /healthz}\n  H -->|200| OK[✅ done]\n  H -->|not 200| F[❌ pipeline fails]","caption":"From git push to running in production"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In the Memos architecture, where does the app container actually run?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"In a public subnet with a public IP","ok":false},{"t":"On ECS Fargate in private subnets, reached only through the load balancer","ok":true},{"t":"Directly on the Application Load Balancer","ok":false},{"t":"In the ECR image registry","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The task sits in <strong>private subnets</strong> with no public IP. Users reach it only through the ALB in the public subnets. ECR just stores the image.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m169--type-html-html-in-the-memos-a"}}]},{"title":"How to use this app","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The four ways to learn here","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>📚 Learn:</strong> short cards, one idea each. Swipe up. The quick checks keep you honest.</li>\n<li><strong>🛠️ Lab:</strong> build the whole Memos platform step by step, typing real commands into a practice terminal.</li>\n<li><strong>🧠 Quiz:</strong> multiple choice from tech-vault and your guides, with explanations. Missed questions come back.</li>\n<li><strong>🎯 Scenarios:</strong> interview-style \"what would you do?\" problems with feedback on every option.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A plan that works","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Little and often:</strong> 15–20 minutes a day beats 3 hours on Sunday.</li>\n<li>Do a Learn module, then its <strong>Lab</strong> stage, then <strong>Quiz</strong> on that topic.</li>\n<li>Say scenario answers <strong>out loud</strong>, as in an interview.</li>\n<li>Revisit <strong>❌ Missed</strong> questions every few days. Spaced repetition is how things stick.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Levels:</strong> modules are tagged <strong>Beginner → Intermediate → Advanced</strong>. Work through them in order the first time.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]}]}]},{"id":"internet","title":"How computers & the internet work","level":"Beginner","icon":"💻","summary":"Servers, clients, IP addresses, ports and what really happens when you open a website.","topic":"Networking","lessons":[{"title":"Computers and servers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What's inside a computer","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every computer, from a laptop to a cloud server, has the same four parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CPU:</strong> does the calculations. Measured in cores (and, in AWS, vCPUs).</li>\n<li><strong>RAM (memory):</strong> fast, temporary workspace. It's wiped when power goes off.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage (disk):</strong> slower, but keeps data permanently.</li>\n<li><strong>Network card:</strong> sends and receives data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>operating system</strong> (Linux, Windows, macOS) shares those parts between running programs.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  APP[\"Programs<br/>(Memos, nginx, bash)\"] --> OS[\"Operating system<br/>(Linux kernel)\"]\n  OS --> CPU[CPU] & RAM[RAM] & DISK[Disk] & NIC[Network]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A server is just a computer that serves","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>server</strong> is a computer that waits for requests and answers them. A <strong>client</strong> is whatever asks: your browser, your phone app, or another server.</p>\n<p>There's nothing magic about server hardware. In the cloud, a \"server\" is usually a <strong>virtual machine</strong> or a <strong>container</strong> running on someone else's hardware.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as 💻 Client (browser)\n  participant S as 🖥️ Server (Memos)\n  C->>S: request: GET /\n  S-->>C: response: 200 OK + HTML page"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the \"server\" is a single container running the <code>memos</code> binary on AWS Fargate, with 0.25 vCPU and 512 MB of RAM.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which part of a computer loses its contents when the power goes off?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The disk","ok":false},{"t":"RAM","ok":true},{"t":"The network card","ok":false},{"t":"An SSD","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>RAM is <strong>volatile</strong>: it's fast but temporary. Disks (HDD or SSD) keep data without power.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m170--type-html-html-which-part-of-"}}]},{"title":"Addresses: IP and ports","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"IP addresses: the street address","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every device on a network has an <strong>IP address</strong> so data can find it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IPv4</strong> looks like <code>203.0.113.10</code>: four numbers from 0 to 255, about 4.3 billion addresses in total.</li>\n<li><strong>IPv6</strong> looks like <code>2001:db8::1</code> and has vastly more addresses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Public</strong> IPs can be reached from the internet. <strong>Private</strong> IPs only work inside a private network and come from three reserved ranges:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Private range</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>10.0.0.0/8</code></td>\n<td>big networks, AWS VPCs (Memos uses <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>172.16.0.0/12</code></td>\n<td>Docker's default networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>192.168.0.0/16</code></td>\n<td>home Wi-Fi routers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"NAT: many private devices, one public IP","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Your home has one public IP, but lots of devices. Your router does <strong>NAT</strong> (Network Address Translation): it swaps private source addresses for its public one on the way out, and remembers which reply belongs to whom.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  P1[\"📱 192.168.1.20\"] --> R\n  P2[\"💻 192.168.1.21\"] --> R\n  R[\"Router (NAT)<br/>public 81.2.3.4\"] --> I((Internet))"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> a <strong>NAT gateway</strong> does the same job in AWS. Private ECS tasks can reach out (to pull images, for example), but nobody on the internet can start a connection in to them.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Ports: the flat number","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An IP address gets data to the right <strong>machine</strong>. A <strong>port</strong> (0–65535) gets it to the right <strong>program</strong> on that machine.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Port</th>\n<th>Used by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>SSH (remote login)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>53</td>\n<td>DNS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>80</td>\n<td>HTTP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>443</td>\n<td>HTTPS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5432 / 3306</td>\n<td>PostgreSQL / MySQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8081</td>\n<td>Memos in this project (Memos' own default is 5230)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An address plus a port, such as <code>10.0.3.25:8081</code>, is called a <strong>socket</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A request reaches the right server but the wrong program answers. What was most likely wrong?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The IP address","ok":false},{"t":"The port number","ok":true},{"t":"The MAC address","ok":false},{"t":"The DNS TTL","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The IP picks the <strong>machine</strong>. The <strong>port</strong> picks the program on it. If the port is wrong, the right server is reached but the wrong program, or no program, answers.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m171--type-html-html-a-request-reac"}}]},{"title":"Opening a website, step by step","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What happens when you type a URL","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Typing <code>https://memos.example.com</code> sets off a chain of steps, and each one is a topic in this course:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant B as Browser\n  participant D as DNS\n  participant L as Load balancer\n  participant A as Memos app\n  B->>D: 1. what IP is memos.example.com?\n  D-->>B: 2. it's 34.x.x.x\n  B->>L: 3. TCP connect to port 443\n  B->>L: 4. TLS handshake (check certificate)\n  B->>L: 5. GET / (encrypted)\n  L->>A: 6. forward to task on :8081\n  A-->>L: 7. 200 OK + page\n  L-->>B: 8. response (encrypted)"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Reading a URL","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"https://memos.example.com:443/api/v1/memos?limit=10\n└─┬─┘   └─────┬────┘ └┬┘ └─────┬─────┘ └──┬───┘\nscheme     host     port    path      query"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Scheme:</strong> how to talk (<code>https</code> means HTTP over TLS).</li>\n<li><strong>Host:</strong> which server, turned into an IP by DNS.</li>\n<li><strong>Port:</strong> optional. Defaults to 443 for https and 80 for http.</li>\n<li><strong>Path:</strong> which resource on the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Query:</strong> extra parameters.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"HTTP: request and response","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>HTTP is plain text with a fixed shape. The client sends a <strong>method</strong> and a <strong>path</strong>. The server replies with a <strong>status code</strong>, <strong>headers</strong> and a <strong>body</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"GET /healthz HTTP/1.1          ← request line\nHost: memos.example.com              ← headers\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK                 ← status line\nContent-Type: text/plain\nService ready.                  ← body"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Common methods: <strong>GET</strong> (read), <strong>POST</strong> (create), <strong>PUT/PATCH</strong> (update), <strong>DELETE</strong> (remove).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Status codes by their first digit","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Range</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2xx</td>\n<td>Success</td>\n<td>200 OK, 201 Created</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3xx</td>\n<td>Go elsewhere</td>\n<td>301 Moved Permanently (Memos' HTTP→HTTPS redirect)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4xx</td>\n<td><strong>Client</strong> made a mistake</td>\n<td>400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorised, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5xx</td>\n<td><strong>Server</strong> failed</td>\n<td>500 Internal Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Unavailable, 504 Timeout</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Tip:</strong> 4xx means look at the request. 5xx means look at the server, and the load balancer's targets.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user gets <strong>403 Forbidden</strong>. What does that tell you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The server crashed","ok":false},{"t":"The page doesn't exist","ok":false},{"t":"The server understood the request but refuses to allow it","ok":true},{"t":"The DNS lookup failed","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>403 is a <strong>4xx client</strong> error: the request reached the server, which decided you aren't allowed. A crash would be 5xx, a missing page 404, and a DNS failure never reaches the server at all.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m172--type-html-html-a-user-gets-40"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does the ALB in Memos send back when someone visits <code>http://memos.example.com</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"200 OK with the page","ok":false},{"t":"301 redirect to the https:// address","ok":true},{"t":"404 Not Found","ok":false},{"t":"502 Bad Gateway","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The HTTP listener on port 80 has a single redirect rule: send <strong>301</strong> to port 443 over HTTPS.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m173--type-html-html-what-does-the-"}}]},{"title":"Clients, servers and APIs","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What an API is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An <strong>API</strong> (Application Programming Interface) is a set of requests a program agrees to answer. Web APIs usually speak HTTP and send <strong>JSON</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"GET /api/v1/memos      →  [{\"id\": 1, \"content\": \"buy milk\"}]\nPOST /api/v1/memos     →  201 Created"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every AWS action, such as \"create a VPC\" or \"push an image\", is an <strong>API call</strong>. The console, the CLI and Terraform are all just different ways of making those calls.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  C[AWS Console] --> API\n  CLI[aws CLI] --> API\n  TF[Terraform] --> API\n  API[\"AWS APIs<br/>(HTTPS + signed requests)\"] --> R[Your resources]"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Terraform, the AWS CLI and the AWS web console all create resources by…</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"editing the same config file","ok":false},{"t":"calling the same AWS APIs","ok":true},{"t":"logging in to the servers over SSH","ok":false},{"t":"running CloudFormation behind the scenes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>They are all clients of the <strong>AWS APIs</strong>. That's why anything you can click in the console can also be automated in code.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m174--type-html-html-terraform-the-"}}]}]},{"id":"linux","title":"Linux fundamentals","level":"Beginner","icon":"🐧","summary":"The terminal, the file system, permissions, processes and services: the ground every DevOps tool stands on.","topic":"Linux","lessons":[{"title":"Linux and the terminal","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Why Linux?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Most servers, almost all containers and nearly every cloud workload run <strong>Linux</strong>. It's free, open source, stable and scriptable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>kernel</strong> is the core: it manages CPU, memory, disks, the network and processes.</li>\n<li>A <strong>distribution</strong> (Ubuntu, Debian, Amazon Linux, Alpine) is the kernel plus tools and a package manager.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the container runs on <strong>Alpine Linux 3.21</strong>, a tiny distribution (about 5 MB) that's popular for containers.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The shell","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <strong>terminal</strong> is the window. The <strong>shell</strong> (usually <code>bash</code>) is the program inside it that reads your commands, runs them and prints the results.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  You[\"⌨️ you type<br/>ls -l /var/log\"] --> Sh[bash shell]\n  Sh -->|asks| K[Linux kernel]\n  K -->|reads| FS[(disk)]\n  Sh --> Out[🖥️ output]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A command has a <strong>name</strong>, <strong>options</strong> (also called flags) and <strong>arguments</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"ls   -l -a    /var/log\n│    └─┬─┘    └───┬───┘\ncmd  options    argument"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Moving around","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pwd</code></td>\n<td>print where you are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -la</code></td>\n<td>list files, including hidden ones, with details</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd /var/log</code></td>\n<td>change directory (<code>cd ..</code> up, <code>cd ~</code> home)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mkdir -p a/b</code></td>\n<td>make folders, creating parents too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cp</code>, <code>mv</code>, <code>rm -r</code></td>\n<td>copy, move or rename, delete (there's no recycle bin!)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cat</code>, <code>less</code>, <code>head</code>, <code>tail -f</code></td>\n<td>read files, and follow a growing log</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <code>rm -rf</code> deletes instantly and permanently. Check the path twice.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows the last lines of a log file and keeps printing new lines as they arrive?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>cat app.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>head -f app.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>tail -f app.log</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>less app.log</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>tail -f</code> <strong>follows</strong> the file. It's the go-to for watching logs live.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m175--type-html-html-which-command-"}}]},{"title":"The file system","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"One tree, starting at /","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Linux has no <code>C:</code> drive. Everything hangs off a single root, <code>/</code>, and even other disks are <strong>mounted</strong> somewhere in the tree.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  R[\"/\"] --> etc[\"/etc<br/>config files\"]\n  R --> var[\"/var<br/>logs, data\"]\n  R --> home[\"/home<br/>users' files\"]\n  R --> tmp[\"/tmp<br/>scratch space\"]\n  R --> usr[\"/usr/bin<br/>programs\"]\n  R --> opt[\"/var/opt/memos<br/>Memos' data\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Folder</th>\n<th>Holds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/etc</code></td>\n<td>system configuration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/var/log</code></td>\n<td>logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/home/&lt;user&gt;</code></td>\n<td>a user's files (<code>/root</code> for root)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/tmp</code></td>\n<td>temporary files, often cleared on reboot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/usr/bin</code>, <code>/usr/local/bin</code></td>\n<td>installed programs (<code>memos</code> lives in <code>/usr/local/bin</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Absolute vs relative paths","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Absolute</strong> paths start at <code>/</code>: <code>/var/opt/memos/memos_prod.db</code></li>\n<li><strong>Relative</strong> paths start where you are: <code>logs/app.log</code></li>\n<li><code>.</code> means \"here\", <code>..</code> means \"one level up\" and <code>~</code> means your home folder.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Everything is a file","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In Linux, nearly everything looks like a file: devices (<code>/dev/sda</code>), process information (<code>/proc</code>), even network sockets. That's why simple tools such as <code>cat</code>, <code>grep</code> and <code>&gt;</code> work almost everywhere.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Inodes: a file's ID card","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Each file has an <strong>inode</strong> that stores its metadata: type, permissions, owner, size, timestamps and where its data blocks are on disk. The <strong>file name is not in the inode</strong>. Names live in directories, which map names to inode numbers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ls -i</code> shows inode numbers.</li>\n<li><code>df -i</code> shows inode usage. A disk can be \"full\" because it has <strong>run out of inodes</strong>, even with space left (for example, millions of tiny files).</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Links: hard vs soft","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  H1[name: report.txt] --> I[(inode 4211<br/>data)]\n  H2[\"hard link: copy.txt\"] --> I\n  S[\"symlink: latest.txt\"] -.->|points to the name| H1"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>A <strong>hard link</strong> is a second name for the same inode. The data survives until every name is deleted.</li>\n<li>A <strong>symbolic (soft) link</strong> is a pointer to a path. It breaks if the target is moved or deleted.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A server says \"No space left on device\", but <code>df -h</code> shows 40% free. What should you check next?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"RAM usage with <code>free -h</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Inode usage with <code>df -i</code>","ok":true},{"t":"The CPU load with <code>top</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Open ports with <code>ss -tlnp</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Every file needs an inode. Millions of tiny files can use them all up while disk space remains, and <code>df -i</code> shows it.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m176--type-html-html-a-server-says-"}}]},{"title":"Users and permissions","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Users, groups and root","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every file and process belongs to a <strong>user</strong>. Users can be in <strong>groups</strong>. <strong>root</strong> (user ID 0) can do anything, which is why you avoid using it day to day and use <code>sudo</code> for single commands.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ id\nuid=1001(appuser) gid=1001(appuser) groups=1001(appuser)"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the Dockerfile creates <code>appuser</code> with UID <strong>1001</strong> and runs the app as <code>USER 1001:1001</code>, never as root. If the app is hacked, the attacker isn't root inside the container.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Reading rwx","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"-rwxr-x---  1 appuser appuser  2.1K  run.sh\n│└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘\n│ │  │  └── others: ---  (nothing)\n│ │  └───── group:  r-x  (read, run)\n│ └──────── owner:  rwx  (read, write, run)\n└────────── type: - file, d directory, l link"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>On a <strong>directory</strong>, <code>r</code> means you can list it, <code>w</code> means you can create or delete files in it, and <code>x</code> means you can enter it (<code>cd</code>).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Numbers: chmod 755","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Each permission has a value: <strong>r = 4, w = 2, x = 1</strong>. Add them up for each of owner, group and others.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>755</code></td>\n<td>rwx r-x r-x</td>\n<td>scripts, programs, folders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>644</code></td>\n<td>rw- r-- r--</td>\n<td>normal files</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>600</code></td>\n<td>rw- --- ---</td>\n<td>private keys (SSH refuses keys that are more open than this)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"type":"output","text":"chmod 755 deploy.sh     # set permissions\nchown appuser:appuser /var/opt/memos   # change owner"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>chmod 640 secrets.env</code> give?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Owner read/write/execute, group read, others nothing","ok":false},{"t":"Owner read/write, group read, others nothing","ok":true},{"t":"Everyone can read","ok":false},{"t":"Owner read only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>6 = 4+2 (rw), 4 = r, 0 = nothing. So: owner <strong>rw-</strong>, group <strong>r--</strong>, others <strong>---</strong>.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m177--type-html-html-what-does-chmo"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You can <code>ls</code> a folder but <code>cd</code> into it fails with \"Permission denied\". Which permission is missing?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"r","ok":false},{"t":"w","ok":false},{"t":"x","ok":true},{"t":"s","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>On directories, <strong>x</strong> means \"enter or traverse\". With <code>r</code> but no <code>x</code> you can see the names but can't go in.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m178--type-html-html-you-can-ls-a-f"}}]},{"title":"Processes and services","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What a process is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>process</strong> is a running program. Each has a <strong>PID</strong> (process ID) and a parent. The first process, <strong>PID 1</strong>, starts everything else. On most systems that's <strong>systemd</strong>. In a container it's your app.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"ps aux | head          # all processes\ntop / htop             # live CPU and memory\npgrep memos            # find a PID by name"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Signals: asking vs forcing","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  K1[\"kill PID<br/>SIGTERM (15)\"] -->|\"please stop:<br/>save work, close cleanly\"| P[process]\n  K2[\"kill -9 PID<br/>SIGKILL (9)\"] -->|\"stop NOW,<br/>no clean-up\"| P"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Always try <strong>SIGTERM</strong> first. Use <code>-9</code> only if it won't stop, because SIGKILL can leave half-written files behind.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Exit codes to know:</strong> <code>0</code> success · <code>1</code> general error · <code>127</code> command not found · <code>137</code> = 128+9, killed by SIGKILL, usually <strong>out of memory</strong> · <code>143</code> = 128+15, stopped by SIGTERM.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Zombies and orphans","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>A <strong>zombie</strong> has finished, but its parent hasn't collected its exit status yet. It uses no CPU or memory, only a PID entry. The fix is the parent.</li>\n<li>An <strong>orphan</strong> is a process whose parent died. It gets adopted by PID 1.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Services with systemd","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Long-running background programs (<strong>daemons</strong>) such as web servers are managed by <strong>systemd</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>systemctl status nginx</code></td>\n<td>is it running? recent logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>systemctl start / stop / restart nginx</code></td>\n<td>control it <strong>now</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>systemctl enable nginx</code></td>\n<td>start it <strong>at boot</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>journalctl -u nginx -f</code></td>\n<td>follow that service's logs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <code>start</code> and <code>enable</code> are different: started means running now, enabled means it starts after a reboot. You often need both.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A container stops with <strong>exit code 137</strong>. What is the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The command wasn't found","ok":false},{"t":"It finished successfully","ok":false},{"t":"It was killed with SIGKILL, often for using too much memory","ok":true},{"t":"It was stopped politely with SIGTERM","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>137 = 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). On ECS or Kubernetes that's usually the <strong>out-of-memory</strong> killer. Memos' task has only 512 MB.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m179--type-html-html-a-container-st"}}]},{"title":"Software, disks and logs","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Package managers","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Install software from trusted repositories, not by downloading random files:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distro</th>\n<th>Command</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ubuntu / Debian</td>\n<td><code>apt update &amp;&amp; apt install -y curl</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amazon Linux / RHEL</td>\n<td><code>dnf install -y curl</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alpine (containers)</td>\n<td><code>apk add --no-cache curl</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the runtime image runs <code>apk add --no-cache ca-certificates tzdata</code>. That's the bare minimum for HTTPS calls and correct time zones.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Disk and memory checks","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Command</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Disk full?</td>\n<td><code>df -h</code> (and <code>df -i</code> for inodes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What's using space?</td>\n<td><code>du -sh /var/log/*</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memory?</td>\n<td><code>free -h</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load?</td>\n<td><code>uptime</code> (load averages over 1, 5 and 15 minutes)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Logs","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>systemd services: <code>journalctl -u &lt;service&gt;</code></li>\n<li>Classic files: <code>/var/log/syslog</code> or <code>/var/log/messages</code></li>\n<li>Containers: they write to <strong>stdout/stderr</strong>, which you read with <code>docker logs</code>, or in <strong>CloudWatch Logs</strong> on ECS.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ If a deleted log file is still held open by a process, its space isn't freed until that process restarts or reopens it. <code>lsof | grep deleted</code> finds these.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command installs a package in an Alpine-based container without keeping the package index (so the image stays small)?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>apt install curl</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>yum install curl</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>apk add --no-cache curl</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>brew install curl</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Alpine uses <strong>apk</strong>. <code>--no-cache</code> skips storing the index, which keeps image layers small.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m180--type-html-html-which-command-"}}]}]},{"id":"bash","title":"Bash scripting & automation","level":"Beginner","icon":"📜","summary":"Turn commands into scripts: variables, pipes, conditions, loops, exit codes and safe defaults.","topic":"Linux","lessons":[{"title":"Your first script","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Why script?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Automation is a core DevOps idea: anything you do twice by hand should become a script. Scripts are repeatable, reviewable and fast. GitHub Actions steps are mostly small bash scripts.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"#!/bin/bash\n# The first line (the \"shebang\") says which program runs the script\necho \"Hello from $(hostname) at $(date)\"","file":"hello.sh"},{"type":"output","text":"$ chmod +x hello.sh     # make it executable\n$ ./hello.sh\nHello from ip-10-0-3-25 at Tue Nov  3 16:07:34 UTC 2026"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Variables and quoting","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"name=\"memos\"            # no spaces around =\necho \"$name\"            # use it with $  → memos\necho \"${name}-prod\"     # braces mark where the name ends → memos-prod\ntoday=$(date +%F)       # command substitution: save a command's output"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quotes</th>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"double\"</code></td>\n<td>variables <strong>are</strong> expanded: <code>\"$name\"</code> → memos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'single'</code></td>\n<td>literal text: <code>'$name'</code> → $name</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Always quote variables (<code>\"$file\"</code>). Without quotes, a filename containing spaces splits into several arguments.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does this print? <code>name=memos; echo '$name is ${name}'</code></p>"}],"options":[{"t":"memos is memos","ok":false},{"t":"$name is ${name}","ok":true},{"t":"memos is ${name}","ok":false},{"t":"an error","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>Single quotes</strong> stop all expansion, so the text is printed exactly as typed.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m181--type-html-html-what-does-this"}}]},{"title":"Pipes and redirection","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Streams: stdin, stdout and stderr","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every process has three streams: <strong>0 stdin</strong> (input), <strong>1 stdout</strong> (normal output) and <strong>2 stderr</strong> (errors).</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  IN[\"stdin (0)\"] --> P[process]\n  P --> OUT[\"stdout (1)\"]\n  P --> ERR[\"stderr (2)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syntax</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt; file</code></td>\n<td>stdout to a file (overwrite)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt;&gt; file</code></td>\n<td>append</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd 2&gt; err.log</code></td>\n<td>stderr to a file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt; all.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code></td>\n<td>both streams to one file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt; /dev/null</code></td>\n<td>throw the output away</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Pipes: chain small tools","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>|</code> sends one command's stdout into the next command's stdin. Small tools that each do one thing combine into powerful one-liners:</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"# Top 5 IPs hitting a web server\ncat access.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[cat log] --> B[\"awk<br/>column 1\"] --> C[sort] --> D[\"uniq -c<br/>count\"] --> E[\"sort -rn<br/>biggest first\"] --> F[head -5]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"grep, awk, sed in one line each","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>grep -i error app.log</code>: lines that match, ignoring case (<code>-r</code> recursive, <code>-v</code> invert)</li>\n<li><code>awk '{print $2}'</code>: print column 2</li>\n<li><code>sed 's/old/new/g' file</code>: find and replace</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command sends both normal output and errors into <code>run.log</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>./run.sh 2&gt; run.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>./run.sh &gt; run.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>./run.sh | run.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>./run.sh &gt;&gt; 2 run.log</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>&gt; run.log</code> sends stdout to the file, then <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> sends stderr to wherever stdout is going. Order matters.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m182--type-html-html-which-command-"}}]},{"title":"Logic: exit codes, if and loops","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Exit codes drive everything","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every command returns a number when it finishes: <strong>0 = success</strong>, anything else = failure. <code>$?</code> holds the last one. CI pipelines fail a step whenever a command returns non-zero.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ curl -sf https://memos.example.com/healthz; echo $?\n0\n$ ls /nope; echo $?\nls: /nope: No such file or directory\n2"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>cmd1 &amp;&amp; cmd2</code> runs cmd2 only if cmd1 <strong>succeeded</strong>. <code>cmd1 || cmd2</code> runs cmd2 only if cmd1 <strong>failed</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"if, for, while","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"#!/bin/bash\ncode=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" https://memos.example.com/healthz)\n\nif [ \"$code\" = \"200\" ]; then\n  echo \"✅ healthy\"\nelse\n  echo \"❌ got $code\"; exit 1\nfi\n\nfor env in dev staging prod; do\n  echo \"checking $env\"\ndone\n\ncount=0\nwhile [ $count -lt 3 ]; do count=$((count + 1)); done","file":"check.sh"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the post-deploy health check in <code>deploy.yml</code> is almost exactly this <code>if</code> block. <code>exit 1</code> is what turns the pipeline red.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Functions and arguments","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"deploy() {\n  local env=\"$1\"        # first argument\n  echo \"deploying to $env\"\n}\ndeploy prod"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Script arguments are <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>, …. <code>$#</code> is how many there are, and <code>$@</code> is all of them.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In <code>make build &amp;&amp; make deploy</code>, when does <code>make deploy</code> run?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Only if <code>make build</code> exits with code 0","ok":true},{"t":"Only if <code>make build</code> fails","ok":false},{"t":"Always, after <code>make build</code>","ok":false},{"t":"At the same time as <code>make build</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> means \"and then, if that worked\". <code>||</code> means \"otherwise\".</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m183--type-html-html-in-make-build-"}}]},{"title":"Writing scripts safely","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The safety line: set -euo pipefail","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Bash keeps going after errors by default, which is dangerous in automation. Put this at the top of every script:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Flag</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-e</code></td>\n<td>stop at the first command that fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-u</code></td>\n<td>using an unset variable is an error (catches typos such as <code>$ENVIROMENT</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-o pipefail</code></td>\n<td>a pipeline fails if <strong>any</strong> part fails, not just the last</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Idempotent scripts","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An <strong>idempotent</strong> script gives the same result however many times you run it. That's vital for automation, which gets retried.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"bash","code":"mkdir -p /var/opt/memos          # fine if it already exists\ngrep -q \"^appuser:\" /etc/passwd || adduser appuser   # only if missing"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> <code>build.yml</code> checks whether an image tag already exists in ECR before building, so re-running the pipeline is safe. That's idempotency.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Scheduling with cron","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>crontab -e</code> runs commands on a schedule:</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"# ┌ minute ┌ hour ┌ day of month ┌ month ┌ day of week\n  0        2      *              *       *     /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\n#  → every day at 02:00"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why add <code>set -o pipefail</code> to a script?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To make pipes run faster","ok":false},{"t":"So a failure anywhere in a pipeline makes the whole pipeline fail","ok":true},{"t":"To stop the script printing errors","ok":false},{"t":"So variables don't need quotes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Without it, <code>failing_cmd | grep x</code> reports grep's exit code and hides the failure.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m184--type-html-html-why-add-set-o-"}}]}]},{"id":"networking","title":"Networking for DevOps","level":"Beginner","icon":"🌐","summary":"Layers, CIDR and subnetting, TCP vs UDP, DNS, TLS, and the tools to debug connections.","topic":"Networking","lessons":[{"title":"The layers model","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Why layers?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Networking is split into <strong>layers</strong>, each doing one job and handing off to the next. When something breaks, you work out <strong>which layer</strong> is failing.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  L7[\"7 Application: HTTP, DNS, SSH\"] --> L4[\"4 Transport: TCP / UDP + ports\"]\n  L4 --> L3[\"3 Network: IP addresses, routing\"]\n  L3 --> L2[\"2 Data link: MAC addresses, switches\"]\n  L2 --> L1[\"1 Physical: cables, Wi-Fi\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The full <strong>OSI model</strong> has 7 layers. Layers 5 (session) and 6 (presentation) are mostly folded into the application layer in practice. The simpler <strong>TCP/IP model</strong> uses 4.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Layers in AWS terms","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>AWS example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>ALB</strong> routes by path or hostname, WAF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>NLB</strong>, security group port rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>route tables, subnets, IP addresses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Interview tip:</strong> \"the ALB is layer 7, the NLB is layer 4\" comes up often.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An Application Load Balancer can route <code>/api/*</code> to one service and <code>/</code> to another. At which layer is it working?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Layer 2","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 3","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 4","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 7","ok":true}],"explain":"<p>Paths and hostnames are <strong>HTTP</strong> details, which is layer 7. A layer-4 balancer only sees IPs and ports.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m185--type-html-html-an-application"}}]},{"title":"IP ranges and subnetting","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"CIDR notation","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>10.0.0.0/16</code> describes a <strong>range</strong>. The number after <code>/</code> says how many leading bits are fixed (the network part). The rest are free for hosts.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CIDR</th>\n<th>Fixed bits</th>\n<th>Addresses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/16</code></td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>65,536</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/24</code></td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>256</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/28</code></td>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/32</code></td>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>1 (a single IP)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rule of thumb: <strong>each +1 halves the size</strong>. Addresses = 2^(32 − prefix).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Splitting a VPC into subnets","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  V[\"VPC 10.0.0.0/16 · 65,536 IPs\"] --> A[\"10.0.1.0/24<br/>public · AZ a\"]\n  V --> B[\"10.0.2.0/24<br/>public · AZ b\"]\n  V --> C[\"10.0.3.0/24<br/>private · AZ a\"]\n  V --> D[\"10.0.4.0/24<br/>private · AZ b\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> exactly this split. Four /24s give room to grow, and every range sits inside the VPC's /16 without overlapping.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"AWS keeps 5 addresses per subnet","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In every AWS subnet, 5 IPs are reserved: the network address, <code>.1</code> (the VPC router), <code>.2</code> (DNS), <code>.3</code> (reserved for future use) and the last address (broadcast). So a <code>/24</code> has <strong>256 − 5 = 251</strong> usable addresses.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How many usable IP addresses does a <code>/24</code> subnet have in AWS?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"256","ok":false},{"t":"254","ok":false},{"t":"251","ok":true},{"t":"250","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>256 total minus <strong>5 reserved by AWS</strong>. (On an ordinary network you'd lose 2, giving 254.)</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m186--type-html-html-how-many-usabl"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which CIDR block contains <strong>more</strong> addresses?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/20</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/24</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/26</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/28</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>smaller</strong> prefix means <strong>more</strong> addresses: /20 = 4,096, /24 = 256, /26 = 64, /28 = 16.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m187--type-html-html-which-cidr-blo"}}]},{"title":"TCP, UDP and connections","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"TCP vs UDP","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>TCP</th>\n<th>UDP</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Connection</td>\n<td>handshake first</td>\n<td>just sends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliable?</td>\n<td>yes: ordered, retransmits lost data</td>\n<td>no guarantees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>a bit slower</td>\n<td>faster, lighter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Used by</td>\n<td>HTTP(S), SSH, databases</td>\n<td>DNS lookups, video, games, VoIP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The three-way handshake","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant S as Server :443\n  C->>S: SYN (let's talk)\n  S-->>C: SYN-ACK (ok, let's talk)\n  C->>S: ACK (great)\n  Note over C,S: connection open, so data can flow"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Refused vs timed out","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>These two errors point to <strong>different</strong> problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Connection refused:</strong> the machine was reached, but <strong>nothing is listening</strong> on that port. Is the app down, or listening on the wrong port?</li>\n<li><strong>Timeout:</strong> packets were <strong>dropped on the way</strong>: a firewall, security group, NACL or route table, or the host is down.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Interview tip:</strong> this one difference narrows most \"can't connect\" problems in seconds.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>curl http://10.0.3.25:8081</code> hangs and then times out. What's the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The app crashed","ok":false},{"t":"Nothing is listening on port 8081","ok":false},{"t":"A firewall, security group or route is dropping the traffic","ok":true},{"t":"The DNS name is wrong","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>timeout</strong> means the packets never got a reply. \"Nothing listening\" would give <strong>connection refused</strong> straight away.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m188--type-html-html-curl-http-10-0"}}]},{"title":"DNS: names to addresses","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"How a DNS lookup works","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant B as Browser\n  participant R as Resolver (ISP / 8.8.8.8)\n  participant Root as Root server\n  participant TLD as .com server\n  participant A as Authoritative (Route 53)\n  B->>R: memos.example.com?\n  R->>Root: memos.example.com?\n  Root-->>R: ask the .com servers\n  R->>TLD: memos.example.com?\n  TLD-->>R: ask example.com's name servers\n  R->>A: memos.example.com?\n  A-->>R: 34.x.x.x (TTL 60)\n  R-->>B: 34.x.x.x (cached for the TTL)"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The answer is <strong>cached</strong> for its <strong>TTL</strong> (time to live, in seconds), which is why DNS changes don't apply instantly everywhere.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Record types","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Points a name to</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td>an IPv4 address</td>\n<td><code>memos.example.com → 34.x.x.x</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AAAA</strong></td>\n<td>an IPv6 address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CNAME</strong></td>\n<td>another name</td>\n<td><code>www → example.com</code> (not allowed at the zone apex)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MX</strong></td>\n<td>mail servers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TXT</strong></td>\n<td>text: verification, SPF</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NS</strong></td>\n<td>the zone's name servers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alias</strong> (Route 53 only)</td>\n<td>an AWS resource, <strong>even at the apex</strong></td>\n<td><code>memos.example.com → the ALB</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos use a Route 53 <strong>alias A record</strong> for the load balancer instead of an ordinary A record?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An ALB's IP addresses change, and an alias follows the ALB automatically","ok":true},{"t":"A records can't point to AWS","ok":false},{"t":"Alias records are faster to type","ok":false},{"t":"It's required for HTTPS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>ALBs don't have fixed IPs. An <strong>alias</strong> record targets the ALB itself and always returns its current addresses. It's also free and works at the zone apex.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m189--type-html-html-why-does-memos"}}]},{"title":"HTTPS and TLS","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What TLS gives you","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>TLS</strong> (the S in HTTPS) provides:\n- <strong>Encryption:</strong> nobody on the way can read the traffic.\n- <strong>Integrity:</strong> nobody can change it unnoticed.\n- <strong>Authentication:</strong> the <strong>certificate</strong> proves the server really is <code>memos.example.com</code>.</p>\n<p>A certificate is signed by a <strong>Certificate Authority (CA)</strong> that browsers trust. In AWS, <strong>ACM</strong> issues certificates for free and renews them automatically.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The handshake, simplified","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant B as Browser\n  participant L as ALB (has the ACM certificate)\n  B->>L: hello: I support TLS 1.3 / 1.2\n  L-->>B: here's my certificate for memos.example.com\n  Note over B: checks the signature chain + name + expiry\n  B->>L: key exchange\n  Note over B,L: both now share a secret key\n  B->>L: GET / (encrypted)"}]},{"type":"card","title":"TLS termination","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Terminating</strong> TLS means decrypting at a chosen point. In Memos, TLS ends <strong>at the ALB</strong>. Traffic from the ALB to the task is plain HTTP on 8081, but it stays <strong>inside the private VPC</strong>, and the security group only allows the ALB.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the HTTPS listener uses <code>ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06</code>, which allows TLS 1.3 and 1.2 only.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Where is TLS terminated in the Memos architecture?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Inside the Memos container","ok":false},{"t":"At the Application Load Balancer","ok":true},{"t":"At Route 53","ok":false},{"t":"At the NAT gateway","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The ALB holds the ACM certificate and decrypts. Behind it, traffic travels inside the VPC to port 8081.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m190--type-html-html-where-is-tls-t"}}]},{"title":"Debugging tools","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The network toolbox","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the host reachable?</td>\n<td><code>ping host</code> (often blocked, so not conclusive)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What does DNS say?</td>\n<td><code>dig memos.example.com</code> / <code>nslookup</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Does the port answer?</td>\n<td><code>nc -zv host 443</code> · <code>curl -v https://host</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where does the path break?</td>\n<td><code>traceroute</code> / <code>mtr</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What's listening here?</td>\n<td><code>ss -tlnp</code> (or <code>netstat -tlnp</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What's in the packets?</td>\n<td><code>tcpdump -i eth0 port 443</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A debugging order that works","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  A[\"DNS: does the name resolve?<br/>dig\"] --> B[\"Route: is the host reachable?<br/>traceroute\"]\n  B --> C[\"Port: is it open and listening?<br/>nc · ss\"]\n  C --> D[\"App: does it answer correctly?<br/>curl -v · logs\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Work <strong>bottom-up</strong> and change <strong>one thing at a time</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows which programs are listening on which TCP ports on this machine?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>ping localhost</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>dig localhost</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ss -tlnp</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>traceroute localhost</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>ss -tlnp</code> = <strong>t</strong>cp, <strong>l</strong>istening, <strong>n</strong>umeric, with the <strong>p</strong>rocess. It's the quick way to spot \"listening on 5230, not 8081\".</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m191--type-html-html-which-command-"}}]}]},{"id":"git","title":"Git & GitHub","level":"Beginner","icon":"🌿","summary":"Version control from your first commit to branches, pull requests, fixing mistakes and keeping secrets out.","topic":"Git","lessons":[{"title":"How Git thinks","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Version control in one sentence","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Git</strong> records <strong>snapshots</strong> of your project over time, so you can see what changed, who changed it and why, and go back. It's <strong>distributed</strong>: every clone holds the full history.</p>\n<p><strong>GitHub</strong> hosts Git repositories online and adds pull requests, reviews, issues and <strong>Actions</strong> (CI/CD).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The three areas","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  W[\"Working directory<br/>files you edit\"] -->|git add| S[\"Staging area<br/>what goes in the next commit\"]\n  S -->|git commit| R[\"Local repository<br/>saved history\"]\n  R -->|git push| GH[\"Remote (GitHub)\"]\n  GH -->|git pull| W"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Your first loop","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"git clone https://github.com/your-username/memos-aws-infra.git\ncd memos-aws-infra\n# …edit files…\ngit status                 # what changed?\ngit diff                   # exactly how?\ngit add infra/main.tf      # stage it\ngit commit -m \"Add VPC module\"\ngit push                   # share it\ngit log --oneline          # history"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You edited a file and ran <code>git commit</code>, but the change isn't in the commit. What did you forget?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git push</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git add</code> (staging the file)","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git pull</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git init</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Commits only include what's <strong>staged</strong>. Run <code>git add &lt;file&gt;</code> first, or <code>git commit -a</code> for files Git already tracks.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m192--type-html-html-you-edited-a-f"}}]},{"title":"Branches and pull requests","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Branches are cheap pointers","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>branch</strong> is a movable label pointing at a commit. You work on a branch so <code>main</code> stays stable.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"gitGraph\n  commit id: \"init\"\n  commit id: \"vpc\"\n  branch feature/alb\n  checkout feature/alb\n  commit id: \"alb\"\n  commit id: \"https\"\n  checkout main\n  merge feature/alb id: \"merge PR\"\n  commit id: \"ecs\""},{"type":"output","text":"git switch -c feature/alb     # create and move to a new branch\ngit push -u origin feature/alb"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The pull-request flow","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  B[branch] --> C[commits] --> PR[open a pull request]\n  PR --> CI[CI checks run]\n  PR --> R[teammate reviews]\n  CI & R --> M[merge to main]\n  M --> D[deploy]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Protect <code>main</code> with <strong>branch protection</strong>: require pull requests, passing checks and an approving review. Nobody pushes straight to production.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Merge vs rebase","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Merge</strong> joins histories with a merge commit. It keeps the true history.</li>\n<li><strong>Rebase</strong> replays your commits on top of the latest <code>main</code>, giving a straight line.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>Golden rule:</strong> only rebase <strong>your own</strong> unshared branches. Rebasing shared history rewrites commits other people have.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Merge conflicts","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>conflict</strong> happens when two branches change the same lines. Git marks the file:</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"<<<<<<< HEAD\ndesired_count = 1\n=======\ndesired_count = 2\n>>>>>>> feature/ha"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Edit it to the correct result, delete the markers, then <code>git add</code> the file and <code>git commit</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When is <code>git rebase</code> a bad idea?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"On your local feature branch before opening a PR","ok":false},{"t":"On a branch other people have already pulled","ok":true},{"t":"To update your branch with the latest main","ok":false},{"t":"To tidy up commits before review","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Rebasing <strong>rewrites</strong> commits. If others built on the old ones, their history no longer matches yours.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m193--type-html-html-when-is-git-re"}}]},{"title":"Fixing mistakes","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Undo, safely","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Command</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Throw away unstaged edits to a file</td>\n<td><code>git restore file</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unstage a file</td>\n<td><code>git restore --staged file</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fix the last commit (not pushed yet)</td>\n<td><code>git commit --amend</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo a commit that's <strong>already pushed</strong></td>\n<td><code>git revert &lt;sha&gt;</code>, which adds a new commit undoing it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Move the branch back (local only)</td>\n<td><code>git reset --hard &lt;sha&gt;</code> ⚠️ deletes work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Park work-in-progress</td>\n<td><code>git stash</code> / <code>git stash pop</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ On shared branches, <strong>revert, don't reset</strong>. Reset rewrites history.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"fetch vs pull","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>git fetch</code> downloads new commits but <strong>doesn't change</strong> your files.</li>\n<li><code>git pull</code> = fetch <strong>+</strong> merge (or rebase) into your branch.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":".gitignore","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>.gitignore</code> lists files Git should never track: <code>.terraform/</code>, <code>*.tfstate</code>, <code>.env</code>, build outputs.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>A classic bug:</strong> the pattern <code>memos</code> (meant for the built binary) also matches the folder <code>cmd/memos/</code>, so <code>main.go</code> is never committed and the build fails in CI. The fix is <code>/memos</code>. A leading <code>/</code> anchors the pattern to the repo root.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A bad commit was pushed to <code>main</code> an hour ago and teammates have pulled it. What's the safe way to undo it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git reset --hard HEAD~1</code> then <code>git push --force</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git revert &lt;sha&gt;</code> then push","ok":true},{"t":"Delete the repository and re-create it","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git stash</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>revert</code> creates a <strong>new</strong> commit that reverses the bad one. History stays intact for everyone.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m194--type-html-html-a-bad-commit-w"}}]},{"title":"Secrets and Git","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Never commit secrets","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Git keeps <strong>everything forever</strong>. Deleting a secret in a later commit doesn't remove it from history, and bots scan public repos for keys within minutes.</p>\n<p>If a secret is committed:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[\"1. Revoke / rotate it NOW\"] --> B[\"2. Check logs for misuse\"]\n  B --> C[\"3. Remove it from history<br/>(git filter-repo / BFG)\"]\n  C --> D[\"4. Prevent it next time<br/>(secret scanning, pre-commit hooks)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the pipeline uses <strong>OIDC</strong>, so there are <strong>no AWS keys to leak</strong>. The only GitHub secret is a role ARN, which is not a credential.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An AWS access key was pushed to a public repo. What's the <strong>first</strong> thing to do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delete the file and push again","ok":false},{"t":"Make the repo private","ok":false},{"t":"Deactivate and rotate the key in AWS immediately","ok":true},{"t":"Rewrite the Git history","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Assume it's already stolen. <strong>Revoke it first</strong>, then clean up history. Deleting the file leaves the key in history.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m195--type-html-html-an-aws-access-"}}]}]},{"id":"docker","title":"Docker & containers","level":"Beginner","icon":"🐳","summary":"Images, containers, Dockerfiles, multi-stage builds, ports, volumes and container security, using Memos' real Dockerfile.","topic":"Docker","lessons":[{"title":"Why containers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"\"Works on my machine\"","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Apps depend on a runtime version, libraries, OS packages and config. Differences between laptops and servers cause bugs. A <strong>container</strong> packages the app <strong>with everything it needs</strong>, so it runs the same everywhere.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Containers vs virtual machines","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  subgraph VM[\"Virtual machines\"]\n    direction TB\n    a1[App A] --> g1[Guest OS] \n    a2[App B] --> g2[Guest OS]\n    g1 & g2 --> H1[Hypervisor] --> HW1[Hardware]\n  end\n  subgraph CT[\"Containers\"]\n    direction TB\n    c1[App A] & c2[App B] --> E[Container runtime] --> K[\"Host OS kernel (shared)\"] --> HW2[Hardware]\n  end"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>VM</th>\n<th>Container</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Includes</td>\n<td>a whole OS</td>\n<td>app + libraries only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starts in</td>\n<td>minutes</td>\n<td>seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size</td>\n<td>GBs</td>\n<td>MBs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Isolation</td>\n<td>strong (own kernel)</td>\n<td>process-level (<strong>shared kernel</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"What a container really is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A container is just a <strong>Linux process</strong> with two kernel features:\n- <strong>Namespaces</strong> give it its own view of processes, network, hostname and files.\n- <strong>cgroups</strong> limit its CPU and memory.</p>\n<p>That's why it starts as fast as a process, and why it's <strong>killed if it goes over its memory limit</strong> (exit 137).</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do containers share that virtual machines don't?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The application code","ok":false},{"t":"The host operating system's kernel","ok":true},{"t":"Their IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"Their file system","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Containers share the host <strong>kernel</strong>. VMs each boot their own OS on a hypervisor. That's why containers are lighter but isolate a little less strongly.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m196--type-html-html-what-do-contai"}}]},{"title":"Images and Dockerfiles","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Image vs container","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>An <strong>image</strong> is a read-only template: the app and its file system, built from a Dockerfile.</li>\n<li>A <strong>container</strong> is a <strong>running instance</strong> of an image. One image can run as many containers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Like a <strong>class</strong> and its <strong>objects</strong>, or a <strong>recipe</strong> and the <strong>meals</strong> made from it.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  DF[Dockerfile] -->|docker build| IMG[(Image)]\n  IMG -->|docker push| REG[(Registry: ECR / Docker Hub)]\n  REG -->|docker pull| IMG2[(Image)]\n  IMG2 -->|docker run| C1[Container 1] & C2[Container 2]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Layers and caching","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Each Dockerfile instruction creates a <strong>layer</strong>. Layers are cached: if nothing changed, Docker reuses the layer. So put things that change <strong>rarely</strong> first and things that change <strong>often</strong> last.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"dockerfile","code":"# ✅ good: dependencies cached until package.json changes\nCOPY package.json pnpm-lock.yaml ./\nRUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile\nCOPY . .            # source changes only rebuild from here"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The core instructions","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instruction</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FROM</code></td>\n<td>base image to start from</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>WORKDIR</code></td>\n<td>set the working folder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COPY</code></td>\n<td>copy files in (prefer it to <code>ADD</code>, which can also unpack and download)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RUN</code></td>\n<td>run a command <strong>at build time</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ENV</code></td>\n<td>set environment variables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EXPOSE</code></td>\n<td>document the port (it doesn't publish it)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>USER</code></td>\n<td>which user runs the process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CMD</code> / <code>ENTRYPOINT</code></td>\n<td>what runs <strong>when the container starts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"CMD vs ENTRYPOINT vs RUN","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>RUN</code> runs <strong>once, during the build</strong>: <code>RUN apk add curl</code>.</li>\n<li><code>ENTRYPOINT</code> is the main executable: <code>ENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]</code>.</li>\n<li><code>CMD</code> gives default arguments, which can be overridden at <code>docker run</code>.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which instruction runs when the container <strong>starts</strong>, rather than when the image is built?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>RUN</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>COPY</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ENTRYPOINT</code> / <code>CMD</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>FROM</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>RUN</code> happens during <code>docker build</code>. <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>/<code>CMD</code> define the process that runs each time a container starts.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m197--type-html-html-which-instruct"}}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds (Memos)","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The problem: huge images","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Building Memos needs Node (frontend), Go (backend), compilers and source code. <strong>Running</strong> it needs only the compiled binary. A single-stage image ships everything, which is slow to pull and has a larger <strong>attack surface</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Build</th>\n<th>Disk usage</th>\n<th>Content size</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Single-stage</td>\n<td>2.79 GB</td>\n<td>633 MB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multi-stage</strong></td>\n<td><strong>75.8 MB</strong></td>\n<td><strong>19.7 MB</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>About <strong>97% smaller</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Three stages","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph S1[\"Stage 1: frontend (node:22-alpine)\"]\n    W[pnpm install<br/>pnpm release] --> DIST[dist/ assets]\n  end\n  subgraph S2[\"Stage 2: backend (golang:1.25-alpine)\"]\n    GO[\"go build<br/>CGO_ENABLED=0\"] --> BIN[/memos binary/]\n  end\n  subgraph S3[\"Stage 3: runtime (alpine:3.21)\"]\n    R[\"binary + CA certs + tzdata<br/>USER 1001 · port 8081\"]\n  end\n  DIST -->|COPY --from=frontend| GO\n  BIN -->|COPY --from=backend| R"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Only <strong>stage 3</strong> becomes the final image. Stages 1 and 2 are thrown away.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The full Dockerfile (lightly trimmed)","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"dockerfile","code":"FROM node:22-alpine AS frontend\nWORKDIR /build/web\nRUN npm install --global pnpm@10.24.0\nCOPY app/web/package.json app/web/pnpm-lock.yaml ./\nRUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile\nCOPY app/web/ ./\nRUN pnpm release\n\nFROM golang:1.25-alpine AS backend\nWORKDIR /build\nCOPY app/go.mod app/go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY app/ ./\nCOPY --from=frontend /build/server/router/frontend/dist ./server/router/frontend/dist\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o /memos ./cmd/memos\n\nFROM alpine:3.21 AS runtime\nRUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates tzdata && \\\n    addgroup -S -g 1001 appuser && adduser -S -D -u 1001 -G appuser appuser\nCOPY --from=backend /memos /usr/local/bin/memos\nENV MEMOS_PORT=8081 MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos\nUSER 1001:1001\nEXPOSE 8081\nENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]","file":"Dockerfile"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Why CGO_ENABLED=0","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> builds a <strong>static</strong> Go binary that doesn't need C libraries at runtime, so it runs on a bare Alpine image. <code>-ldflags=\"-s -w\"</code> strips debug symbols, making it smaller again.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a multi-stage build, what ends up in the final image?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Every stage, stacked together","ok":false},{"t":"Only the last stage, plus anything it copies from earlier stages","ok":true},{"t":"Only the first stage","ok":false},{"t":"The build cache","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Earlier stages are <strong>build-only</strong>. <code>COPY --from=&lt;stage&gt;</code> pulls just the artefacts you need into the final stage.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m198--type-html-html-in-a-multi-sta"}}]},{"title":"Running containers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"docker run, decoded","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"docker run -d --name memos -p 8081:8081 -v memos-data:/var/opt/memos memos:dev\n           │  │            │            │                              └ image:tag\n           │  │            │            └ volume: keep data outside the container\n           │  │            └ publish host port 8081 → container port 8081\n           │  └ name it\n           └ detached (in the background)"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Ports: -p host:container","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  B[\"browser<br/>localhost:8081\"] --> H[\"host port 8081\"] -->|\"-p 8081:8081\"| C[\"container port 8081<br/>memos\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>EXPOSE</code> in the Dockerfile is only documentation. <code>-p</code> actually opens the port.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Containers are disposable: volumes","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A container's file system <strong>disappears when the container is removed</strong>. Data that must survive goes in a <strong>volume</strong> (managed by Docker) or a <strong>bind mount</strong> (a host folder).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> on ECS the task has no volume, so the SQLite database at <code>/var/opt/memos</code> is <strong>lost whenever the task is replaced</strong>. That's the project's biggest known limitation. The fixes (EFS or RDS) come later.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Everyday commands","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker ps -a</code></td>\n<td>list containers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker logs -f memos</code></td>\n<td>follow logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker exec -it memos sh</code></td>\n<td>open a shell inside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker stop / rm memos</code></td>\n<td>stop / remove</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker images</code> / <code>docker rmi</code></td>\n<td>list / remove images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>docker system prune</code></td>\n<td>clean up unused data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You run <code>docker run -p 9000:8081 memos:dev</code>. Which URL reaches the app from your laptop?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>http://localhost:8081</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>http://localhost:9000</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>http://localhost:5230</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>http://memos:8081</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>-p HOST:CONTAINER</code>, so host port <strong>9000</strong> forwards to the container's 8081.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m199--type-html-html-you-run-docker"}}]},{"title":"Compose and container security","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Docker Compose","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Compose</strong> defines several containers that work together in one YAML file, which is ideal for local development:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"services:\n  memos:\n    build: .\n    ports: [\"8081:8081\"]\n    environment:\n      MEMOS_DRIVER: postgres\n      MEMOS_DSN: postgres://memos:secret@db:5432/memos?sslmode=disable\n    depends_on: [db]\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    environment: { POSTGRES_USER: memos, POSTGRES_PASSWORD: secret }\n    volumes: [\"pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\"]\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:","file":"compose.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>docker compose up -d</code> starts both. Services reach each other <strong>by name</strong> (<code>db</code>).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Container security checklist","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Use <strong>small, trusted base images</strong> (alpine, distroless) and pin versions.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't run as root:</strong> <code>USER 1001</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>No secrets in images.</strong> They stay in the layers forever. Inject them at runtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Scan images</strong> (ECR scan-on-push, Trivy) and rebuild often for patches.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>immutable tags</strong> (git SHA), not <code>latest</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> non-root user, Alpine runtime, scan on push, immutable SHA tags.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why is baking an API key into a Docker image a bad idea, even if you delete it in a later layer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It makes the image too large","ok":false},{"t":"Earlier layers still contain it, and anyone who can pull the image can extract it","ok":true},{"t":"Docker refuses to build it","ok":false},{"t":"It slows the container down","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Layers are <strong>additive</strong>. A file deleted later still exists in the earlier layer. Inject secrets at runtime instead.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m200--type-html-html-why-is-baking-"}}]}]},{"id":"aws-foundations","title":"Cloud & AWS foundations","level":"Beginner","icon":"☁️","summary":"What the cloud is, regions and AZs, shared responsibility, a safe account setup and IAM, the foundation of AWS security.","topic":"AWS","lessons":[{"title":"What the cloud is","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Renting computers by the second","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Cloud computing</strong> means renting computing power, storage and services over the internet, on demand, and paying for what you use. You don't buy hardware, and you can scale up or down in minutes.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"IaaS, PaaS, SaaS","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  I[\"IaaS · EC2<br/>you run: OS + app\"] -->|less to manage| P[\"PaaS · Fargate, Lambda<br/>you run: app + data\"] -->|less again| S[\"SaaS · Gmail<br/>you: just use it\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>You manage</th>\n<th>AWS example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IaaS</td>\n<td>OS, runtime, app</td>\n<td>EC2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PaaS</td>\n<td>app and data</td>\n<td>Elastic Beanstalk, Fargate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SaaS</td>\n<td>nothing technical</td>\n<td>WorkMail, Gmail</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> Fargate sits close to PaaS. You give it a container, and AWS runs and patches the servers underneath.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Why companies move to the cloud","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Elasticity:</strong> scale with demand, rather than buying for the peak.</li>\n<li><strong>Pay as you go:</strong> an operating cost instead of an upfront capital cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Speed:</strong> a new environment in minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Global reach:</strong> deploy near users anywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Managed services:</strong> databases, queues and DNS without running them yourself.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>On <strong>EC2</strong>, who is responsible for patching the operating system?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"AWS","ok":false},{"t":"You (the customer)","ok":true},{"t":"Nobody, because it patches itself","ok":false},{"t":"The hypervisor","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>EC2 is IaaS, so the OS is <strong>yours</strong> to patch. On Fargate, AWS patches the hosts. That's the shared responsibility model.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m201--type-html-html-on-ec2-who-is-"}}]},{"title":"Regions, AZs and shared responsibility","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Global infrastructure","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  G[AWS global] --> R1[\"Region eu-west-1 (Ireland)\"] & R2[\"Region eu-west-2 (London)\"]\n  R1 --> A1[\"AZ eu-west-1a<br/>data centre(s)\"] & A2[\"AZ eu-west-1b\"] & A3[\"AZ eu-west-1c\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>A <strong>Region</strong> is a geographic area, such as eu-west-1 in Ireland. Regions are independent of each other.</li>\n<li>An <strong>Availability Zone (AZ)</strong> is one or more separate data centres inside a Region, with their own power and network. Every Region has <strong>3 or more</strong>, joined by fast private links.</li>\n<li><strong>Edge locations</strong> cache content close to users (CloudFront).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> everything is in <strong>eu-west-1</strong> across <strong>two AZs</strong> (a and b), so one data centre failing doesn't take down the network.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Choosing a Region","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Compliance:</strong> where data is legally allowed to live.</li>\n<li><strong>Latency:</strong> how close it is to your users.</li>\n<li><strong>Service availability:</strong> not every service exists in every Region.</li>\n<li><strong>Price:</strong> prices differ by Region.</li>\n</ol>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The shared responsibility model","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  C[\"🧑 YOU: security IN the cloud<br/>your data · IAM · security groups · OS patches on EC2 · encryption choices · app code\"]\n  A[\"☁️ AWS: security OF the cloud<br/>data centres · hardware · network · hypervisor · managed-service internals\"]\n  C --- A"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ AWS won't stop you making an S3 bucket public or opening port 22 to the world. Those are <strong>your</strong> responsibility.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A company must keep customer data inside the UK. Which factor decides their Region first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Price","ok":false},{"t":"Latency","ok":false},{"t":"Compliance (data residency)","ok":true},{"t":"Number of AZs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Legal requirements come <strong>before</strong> speed or cost. Here that means eu-west-2 (London).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m202--type-html-html-a-company-must"}}]},{"title":"A safe AWS account","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The root user","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The email you sign up with is the <strong>root user</strong>, which has unlimited power, including closing the account. Use it almost never.</p>\n<p><strong>First four things to do in a new account:</strong>\n1. Turn on <strong>MFA</strong> for root.\n2. Set a <strong>budget alert</strong> (AWS Budgets) so a mistake doesn't become a huge bill.\n3. Create a separate admin identity (<strong>IAM Identity Center</strong>) and use that day to day.\n4. <strong>Never</strong> create access keys for root.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Surprise bills: what costs money even when idle","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>NAT gateways:</strong> charged per hour <strong>plus</strong> per GB.</li>\n<li><strong>Public IPv4 addresses</strong>, including Elastic IPs.</li>\n<li><strong>Load balancers</strong>, even with no traffic.</li>\n<li><strong>EBS volumes</strong> of stopped instances, and snapshots.</li>\n<li>Data transfer <strong>out</strong> to the internet and <strong>between AZs</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the two NAT gateways and the ALB are the biggest fixed costs. That's why <code>destroy.yml</code> exists, to tear everything down when you're not using it.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these keeps costing money even when nobody is using your app?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A Lambda function with no invocations","ok":false},{"t":"An empty S3 bucket","ok":false},{"t":"A NAT gateway","ok":true},{"t":"An IAM role","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>NAT gateways bill <strong>per hour</strong> whether or not traffic flows, and per GB on top. Idle Lambda, empty buckets and IAM cost nothing.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m203--type-html-html-which-of-these"}}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The building blocks","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  U[👤 User<br/>a person, long-term credentials] --> P\n  G[👥 Group<br/>users with the same job] --> P\n  R[🎭 Role<br/>assumed temporarily by services or CI] --> P\n  P[\"📜 Policy (JSON)<br/>Allow / Deny · actions · resources\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Users</strong> are people, with passwords and maybe access keys.</li>\n<li><strong>Groups</strong> are bundles of users: attach policies once.</li>\n<li><strong>Roles</strong> have <strong>no password</strong>. A trusted principal (an AWS service, GitHub Actions, another account) <strong>assumes</strong> a role and gets <strong>temporary</strong> credentials.</li>\n<li><strong>Policies</strong> are JSON documents that grant or deny permissions.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Reading a policy","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Action\": [\"ecr:GetAuthorizationToken\", \"ecr:BatchGetImage\"],\n    \"Resource\": \"*\"\n  }]\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Each statement says <strong>Effect</strong> (Allow or Deny), <strong>Action</strong> (which API calls), <strong>Resource</strong> (which ARNs) and, optionally, <strong>Condition</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"How AWS decides: the evaluation order","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  S([request]) --> D{\"Any explicit Deny?\"}\n  D -->|yes| X[\"❌ Denied\"]\n  D -->|no| A{\"Any Allow?\"}\n  A -->|yes| OK[\"✅ Allowed\"]\n  A -->|no| X2[\"❌ Denied (implicit)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Everything is denied by default. An Allow grants access. An explicit Deny always wins.</strong></p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Roles and STS","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When something assumes a role, <strong>STS</strong> (Security Token Service) hands out <strong>temporary credentials</strong> that expire, typically within an hour. Nothing long-lived can leak.</p>\n<p>A role has two policies:\n- the <strong>trust policy</strong>: <em>who</em> may assume it\n- the <strong>permissions policy</strong>: <em>what</em> it can do once assumed</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> ECS tasks assume <code>memos-ecs-execution-role</code> (pull from ECR, write logs). GitHub Actions assumes <code>github-actions-role</code> through OIDC. There are no long-lived keys anywhere.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Least privilege","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Grant <strong>only</strong> the permissions needed, on <strong>only</strong> the resources needed, for <strong>only</strong> as long as needed. Start small and add permissions when something is denied, rather than starting with admin.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Note on the example:</strong> the first version of the Memos CI role uses <code>AdministratorAccess</code>. That works, but it isn't least privilege. Tightening it is a great interview talking point.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user is in a group whose policy <strong>allows</strong> <code>s3:*</code>, and also has a policy that <strong>denies</strong> <code>s3:DeleteBucket</code>. Can they delete a bucket?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Yes, because the group Allow covers it","ok":false},{"t":"No, because an explicit Deny always wins","ok":true},{"t":"Yes, but only buckets they created","ok":false},{"t":"It depends which policy was attached first","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Explicit <strong>Deny</strong> beats any Allow, every time, whatever the order.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m204--type-html-html-a-user-is-in-a"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why use an IAM <strong>role</strong> instead of an IAM user with access keys for an app or a pipeline?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Roles are cheaper","ok":false},{"t":"Roles give temporary credentials that expire, so there are no long-lived keys to leak","ok":true},{"t":"Users can't call AWS APIs","ok":false},{"t":"Roles skip permission checks","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Long-lived keys are the most common source of AWS breaches. A role's credentials come from STS and <strong>expire</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m205--type-html-html-why-use-an-iam"}}]},{"title":"Using AWS: console, CLI, SDK","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Three ways in, one API","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Console:</strong> the web UI. Good for exploring and learning. Bad for repeatable work.</li>\n<li><strong>CLI:</strong> <code>aws</code> commands in a terminal or script.</li>\n<li><strong>SDKs and IaC:</strong> code (Python boto3, Terraform) that calls the same APIs.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ aws configure sso                       # log in through IAM Identity Center\n$ aws sts get-caller-identity             # who am I?\n{\n  \"UserId\": \"AROA…:you\",\n  \"Account\": \"123456789012\",\n  \"Arn\": \"arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/AdminAccess/you\"\n}"}]},{"type":"card","title":"ARNs: AWS's universal IDs","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every resource has an <strong>ARN</strong> (Amazon Resource Name):</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"arn:aws:ecs:eu-west-1:123456789012:service/memos-cluster/memos-service\n    │   │   │         │            └ resource\n    │   │   │         └ account ID\n    │   │   └ region\n    │   └ service\n    └ partition"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command tells you which AWS account and identity your CLI is currently using?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>aws whoami</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>aws iam list-users</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>aws configure list-accounts</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>sts get-caller-identity</code> is the first thing to run when \"access denied\" looks wrong. It's often the wrong account or role.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m206--type-html-html-which-command-"}}]}]},{"id":"compute-storage","title":"Compute, storage & databases on AWS","level":"Intermediate","icon":"🖥️","summary":"EC2, Auto Scaling, Lambda, S3, EBS vs EFS, and RDS. These are the building blocks around the Memos design.","topic":"AWS","lessons":[{"title":"EC2: virtual servers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What EC2 is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>EC2</strong> rents you virtual machines (<strong>instances</strong>). You choose:\n- an <strong>AMI</strong> (Amazon Machine Image): the OS template to start from\n- an <strong>instance type</strong>: CPU and RAM, such as <code>t3.micro</code> or <code>m7g.large</code>\n- a <strong>subnet</strong>, <strong>security groups</strong> and a <strong>key pair</strong> or SSM for access</p>\n<p>Instance families: <strong>t</strong> (burstable, cheap), <strong>m</strong> (general), <strong>c</strong> (compute), <strong>r</strong> (memory). A <code>g</code> suffix (as in m7<strong>g</strong>) means AWS's ARM Graviton chips, which are often cheaper for the same work.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Paying for EC2","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n<th>Saving</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On-Demand</td>\n<td>short or unpredictable work</td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Savings Plans / Reserved</td>\n<td>steady 1–3 year use</td>\n<td>up to ~72%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spot</td>\n<td>interruptible work (batch, CI runners)</td>\n<td>up to ~90%, but can be taken back with 2 minutes' notice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Reaching an instance safely","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Opening SSH (22) to the internet is risky. Better options:\n- <strong>SSM Session Manager:</strong> a shell through the AWS API, with no open ports and no keys\n- a <strong>bastion</strong> host with SSH restricted to your IP\n- immutable servers you <strong>never log in to</strong>: replace them instead of fixing them</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A nightly batch job can safely be restarted if interrupted. Which pricing option saves the most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"On-Demand","ok":false},{"t":"Reserved Instances","ok":false},{"t":"Spot Instances","ok":true},{"t":"Dedicated Hosts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>Spot</strong> uses spare capacity at up to ~90% off. It can be reclaimed, which is fine for work that can restart.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m207--type-html-html-a-nightly-batc"}}]},{"title":"Scaling: elasticity and Auto Scaling","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Vertical vs horizontal","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph V[\"Vertical: scale UP\"]\n    s1[small] --> s2[BIGGER]\n  end\n  subgraph H[\"Horizontal: scale OUT\"]\n    h1[server] --> h2[server] & h3[server] & h4[server]\n  end"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Vertical:</strong> a bigger machine. Simple, but it has limits and is still a single point of failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Horizontal:</strong> more machines behind a load balancer. This is how cloud apps scale.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Auto Scaling groups","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An <strong>Auto Scaling Group (ASG)</strong> keeps between <strong>min</strong> and <strong>max</strong> instances running, replaces unhealthy ones, and scales using policies:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target tracking</td>\n<td>keep average CPU at <strong>60%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Step</td>\n<td>+2 instances if CPU &gt; 80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scheduled</td>\n<td>10 instances every weekday 09:00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predictive</td>\n<td>learns daily patterns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Don't scale at 100% CPU. New capacity takes minutes to arrive, so aim for <strong>60–70%</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Traffic always peaks Wednesdays and Fridays from 9am to 7pm. What's the simplest way to have capacity ready in advance?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Scale when CPU reaches 100%","ok":false},{"t":"A scheduled scaling action before 9am on those days","ok":true},{"t":"Buy the biggest instance type","ok":false},{"t":"Manually add servers each morning","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Known, repeating peaks → <strong>scheduled scaling</strong>. You can still add target tracking for anything unexpected.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m208--type-html-html-traffic-always"}}]},{"title":"Serverless with Lambda","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Functions, not servers","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Lambda</strong> runs your code <strong>in response to events</strong>, scales automatically and bills per request and per millisecond. There are no servers to manage, and it costs nothing when idle.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  E1[S3 upload] --> L((λ Lambda))\n  E2[API Gateway request] --> L\n  E3[EventBridge schedule] --> L\n  E4[SQS message] --> L\n  L --> O[(DynamoDB / S3 / anything)]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Limits to know: <strong>15 minutes</strong> maximum runtime per invocation, cold starts on first use, and it's stateless.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Lambda vs containers vs EC2","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Lambda</th>\n<th>Fargate / ECS</th>\n<th>EC2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit</td>\n<td>function</td>\n<td>container</td>\n<td>VM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runs</td>\n<td>per event, ≤ 15 min</td>\n<td>long-running</td>\n<td>long-running</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Idle cost</td>\n<td>£0</td>\n<td>per task</td>\n<td>per instance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good for</td>\n<td>glue, events, spiky APIs</td>\n<td>web apps (Memos)</td>\n<td>full control, special needs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why wouldn't you run the Memos web app on Lambda as it is?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Lambda can't run Go","ok":false},{"t":"Memos is a long-running server that keeps local SQLite state, which doesn't fit short, stateless functions","ok":true},{"t":"Lambda is more expensive at any scale","ok":false},{"t":"Lambda can't sit behind HTTPS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Lambda suits short, stateless, event-driven code. Memos is a long-running HTTP server with local state, so containers fit better.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m209--type-html-html-why-wouldn-t-y"}}]},{"title":"Storage: S3, EBS, EFS","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Three kinds of storage","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  O[\"🪣 Object: S3<br/>files by key over HTTPS<br/>unlimited, very durable\"]\n  B[\"💽 Block: EBS<br/>a disk for ONE instance<br/>in ONE AZ\"]\n  F[\"📁 File: EFS<br/>a shared network drive (NFS)<br/>many clients, across AZs\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>S3</th>\n<th>EBS</th>\n<th>EFS</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Access</td>\n<td>HTTP API</td>\n<td>attached disk</td>\n<td>network mount</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared?</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>one instance (usually)</td>\n<td>many, across AZs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use</td>\n<td>backups, assets, <strong>Terraform state</strong></td>\n<td>EC2 root disks, databases</td>\n<td>shared files, <strong>container volumes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"S3 essentials","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Buckets</strong> hold <strong>objects</strong> (files) addressed by <strong>key</strong>. Bucket names are globally unique.</li>\n<li><strong>Durability</strong> of 99.999999999% (\"eleven nines\"), so data is almost never lost. <strong>Availability</strong> (can you reach it right now) is a separate figure, 99.99% for Standard.</li>\n<li><strong>Block Public Access</strong>, <strong>versioning</strong>, <strong>encryption</strong> and <strong>lifecycle</strong> rules (move old data to cheaper classes such as Glacier).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the bucket <code>memos-tfstate-example</code> holds Terraform state, with versioning, encryption, public access blocked and a policy that denies non-HTTPS access.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Memos loses its SQLite data whenever the Fargate task is replaced. Which storage could fix that without changing the database?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"S3, mounted as a disk","ok":false},{"t":"EBS, shared across AZs","ok":false},{"t":"EFS, mounted into the task at /var/opt/memos","ok":true},{"t":"Instance store","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>EFS</strong> is a network file system that ECS tasks can mount and that survives task replacement. (A managed database like RDS is the better long-term fix.)</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m210--type-html-html-memos-loses-it"}}]},{"title":"Databases","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Managed databases","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Running a database yourself means handling backups, patching, failover and storage. <strong>Managed</strong> services do it for you.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RDS</strong></td>\n<td>relational (Postgres, MySQL…)</td>\n<td>most apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aurora</strong></td>\n<td>AWS's cloud-built relational DB</td>\n<td>higher scale and availability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DynamoDB</strong></td>\n<td>key-value / NoSQL, serverless</td>\n<td>huge scale, simple access patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ElastiCache</strong></td>\n<td>Redis / Memcached</td>\n<td>caching, sessions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"RDS Multi-AZ vs read replicas","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  App[App] --> P[(Primary · AZ a)]\n  P -. \"synchronous copy\" .-> SB[(Standby · AZ b)]\n  P -. \"asynchronous copy\" .-> RR[(Read replica)]\n  App -. reads .-> RR"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Multi-AZ</strong> gives <strong>availability</strong>: a standby in another AZ takes over automatically. You can't read from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Read replicas</strong> give <strong>scale</strong>: extra copies that serve reads, and can be in another Region.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Where Memos stands","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Memos supports <strong>SQLite, MySQL and Postgres</strong>, chosen with <code>MEMOS_DRIVER</code> and <code>MEMOS_DSN</code>. Today it uses SQLite inside the container.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  T[Memos task<br/>private subnet] -->|5432, from the ECS SG only| DB[(\"RDS Postgres<br/>Multi-AZ<br/>isolated subnets\")]\n  SM[Secrets Manager] -. DSN injected at start .-> T"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>That's the missing <strong>data tier</strong>. Adding it makes the app stateless, so you can run 2+ tasks.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does RDS <strong>Multi-AZ</strong> give you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Faster reads","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic failover to a standby in another AZ","ok":true},{"t":"A copy in another Region","ok":false},{"t":"Lower cost","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Multi-AZ is for <strong>availability</strong>. For faster reads, add <strong>read replicas</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m211--type-html-html-what-does-rds-"}}]}]},{"id":"vpc","title":"AWS networking: VPC","level":"Intermediate","icon":"🏘️","summary":"VPCs, public vs private subnets, internet and NAT gateways, route tables, security groups vs NACLs, and VPC endpoints.","topic":"AWS","lessons":[{"title":"The VPC","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Your own private network","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>VPC</strong> (Virtual Private Cloud) is an isolated network inside AWS. You choose its IP range (CIDR), then carve it into <strong>subnets</strong>, one per AZ.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  subgraph VPC[\"VPC 10.0.0.0/16 · eu-west-1\"]\n    subgraph AZa[\"AZ eu-west-1a\"]\n      P1[\"public 10.0.1.0/24\"]\n      R1[\"private 10.0.3.0/24\"]\n    end\n    subgraph AZb[\"AZ eu-west-1b\"]\n      P2[\"public 10.0.2.0/24\"]\n      R2[\"private 10.0.4.0/24\"]\n    end\n  end"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> this is the exact layout from <code>terraform.tfvars</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A subnet lives in one AZ","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A subnet can't span AZs. For high availability you build <strong>matching subnets in at least two AZs</strong> and spread resources across them.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos create subnets in <strong>two</strong> Availability Zones?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"AWS requires at least two subnets","ok":false},{"t":"So the network (and the ALB) keeps working if one AZ fails","ok":true},{"t":"To get more IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"Because NAT gateways need two subnets","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Spreading across AZs is the basis of <strong>high availability</strong>. ALBs also require subnets in at least two AZs.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m212--type-html-html-why-does-memos"}}]},{"title":"Public vs private subnets","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What makes a subnet public","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It's <strong>not</strong> a checkbox. A subnet is <strong>public</strong> if its <strong>route table</strong> sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an <strong>internet gateway (IGW)</strong>. Otherwise it's <strong>private</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph pub[\"Public route table\"]\n    a1[\"10.0.0.0/16 → local\"]\n    a2[\"0.0.0.0/0 → igw-…\"]\n  end\n  subgraph prv[\"Private route table (per AZ)\"]\n    b1[\"10.0.0.0/16 → local\"]\n    b2[\"0.0.0.0/0 → nat-… (same AZ)\"]\n  end"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <code>local</code> route always exists: everything in the VPC can route to everything else (security groups then decide what's actually allowed).</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Internet gateway vs NAT gateway","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  I((Internet)) <-->|both ways| IGW[Internet gateway]\n  IGW <--> PUB[\"Public subnet<br/>ALB · NAT gateway\"]\n  PRIV[\"Private subnet<br/>ECS task\"] -->|outbound only| NAT[NAT gateway] --> IGW"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>IGW:</strong> two-way internet access for resources with public IPs.</li>\n<li><strong>NAT gateway:</strong> lives in a <strong>public</strong> subnet and lets <strong>private</strong> resources reach <strong>out</strong> (to pull images or call APIs). Nothing from outside can start a connection <strong>in</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"One NAT per AZ, or one total?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>One NAT per AZ</strong> (Memos)</td>\n<td>if an AZ fails, the other AZ still has internet</td>\n<td>2× the cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One shared NAT</td>\n<td>cheaper</td>\n<td>single point of failure, and cross-AZ data charges</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A private subnet's route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to a NAT gateway. What can its instances do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Receive connections from the internet","ok":false},{"t":"Start outbound connections to the internet, but not accept inbound ones","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing: private subnets are fully offline","ok":false},{"t":"Only talk to S3","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>NAT lets traffic <strong>out</strong> and the replies come back. New inbound connections from the internet aren't possible.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m213--type-html-html-a-private-subn"}}]},{"title":"Firewalls: security groups and NACLs","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Security groups: stateful, per resource","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>security group (SG)</strong> is a firewall attached to a resource (an ENI). It's:\n- <strong>Stateful:</strong> if traffic is allowed in, the reply is allowed out automatically.\n- <strong>Allow-only:</strong> there are no deny rules. Anything not allowed is blocked.\n- Able to reference <strong>other security groups</strong> instead of IP ranges.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Memos' security groups","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  W((Internet)) -->|80, 443| ALB[\"ALB SG\"]\n  ALB -->|\"8081 → ECS SG only\"| ECS[\"ECS SG\"]\n  ECS -->|\"443 out (ECR, logs via NAT)\"| O((AWS APIs))"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>ALB SG:</strong> in 80/443 from anywhere. Out <strong>only</strong> to the ECS SG on 8081.</li>\n<li><strong>ECS SG:</strong> in 8081 <strong>only from the ALB SG</strong>. Out 443.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Referencing the <strong>SG</strong> rather than an IP range means any ALB node is allowed, and nothing else is.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"NACLs: stateless, per subnet","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>network ACL</strong> guards a whole <strong>subnet</strong>:\n- <strong>Stateless:</strong> you must allow the return traffic too (on ephemeral ports 1024–65535).\n- Has <strong>allow and deny</strong> rules, checked <strong>in number order</strong>.\n- The default NACL allows everything, and most teams leave it that way and rely on SGs.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Security group</th>\n<th>NACL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attached to</td>\n<td>resource (ENI)</td>\n<td>subnet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State</td>\n<td>stateful</td>\n<td>stateless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rules</td>\n<td>allow only</td>\n<td>allow + deny, numbered</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does the Memos ECS security group allow port 8081 <strong>from the ALB's security group</strong> rather than from <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's required by ECS","ok":false},{"t":"It allows only the load balancer, not every other resource in the VPC","ok":true},{"t":"Security groups can't use CIDR ranges","ok":false},{"t":"It's faster","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>SG-to-SG rules are the tightest option: only traffic from resources in the ALB SG can reach the task.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m214--type-html-html-why-does-the-m"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You need to <strong>block one specific IP address</strong> from reaching a subnet. What should you use?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A security group deny rule","ok":false},{"t":"A network ACL deny rule","ok":true},{"t":"A route table entry","ok":false},{"t":"An IAM policy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Security groups <strong>can't deny</strong>. NACLs have deny rules (WAF is another option for HTTP traffic).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m215--type-html-html-you-need-to-bl"}}]},{"title":"Private access to AWS services","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"VPC endpoints","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Private resources normally reach AWS APIs (ECR, S3, CloudWatch) <strong>through the NAT gateway</strong>, which costs per GB. <strong>VPC endpoints</strong> give a private path instead:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  T[ECS task] --> GW[\"Gateway endpoint<br/>S3 · DynamoDB<br/>FREE\"]\n  T --> IF[\"Interface endpoints<br/>ECR api/dkr · Logs · Secrets Manager<br/>hourly + per GB\"]\n  GW --> S3[(S3)]\n  IF --> AWS[AWS APIs]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Memos improvement:</strong> an S3 gateway endpoint (free) would carry ECR's image layers, which are stored in S3, past the NAT.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Peering, Transit Gateway, VPN","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>VPC peering:</strong> connect two VPCs directly. Their CIDRs <strong>must not overlap</strong>, and it isn't transitive.</li>\n<li><strong>Transit Gateway:</strong> a hub joining many VPCs and on-premises networks.</li>\n<li><strong>Site-to-Site VPN / Direct Connect:</strong> link your office or data centre to AWS.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Plan CIDRs so they never overlap (Memos 10.0.0.0/16, the next environment 10.1.0.0/16 …). Fixing an overlap later is painful.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Flow logs","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>VPC Flow Logs</strong> record accepted and rejected connections (IPs, ports, action). They're great for \"why is this blocked?\" questions and for security investigations.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> flow logs are deliberately <strong>off</strong> to save cost, documented with a <code>tfsec:ignore</code> comment. That's a known, accepted trade-off.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which VPC endpoint type is <strong>free</strong> and covers S3?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Gateway endpoint","ok":true},{"t":"Interface endpoint","ok":false},{"t":"NAT endpoint","ok":false},{"t":"Transit endpoint","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Gateway endpoints (S3 and DynamoDB only) are free route-table entries. Interface endpoints are network interfaces billed per hour.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m216--type-html-html-which-vpc-endp"}}]}]},{"id":"alb-dns-tls","title":"Load balancers, DNS & certificates","level":"Intermediate","icon":"⚖️","summary":"How the ALB, target groups and health checks work, Route 53 and alias records, and ACM certificates with DNS validation.","topic":"AWS","lessons":[{"title":"Load balancers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What a load balancer does","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>load balancer</strong> is the single front door for your app. It spreads requests across healthy backends, ends TLS, and sends traffic only to targets that pass <strong>health checks</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  U((users)) --> LB[Load balancer]\n  LB --> T1[\"✅ target 1\"]\n  LB --> T2[\"✅ target 2\"]\n  LB -.-x T3[\"❌ target 3<br/>unhealthy: no traffic\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Types of AWS load balancer","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Strengths</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ALB</strong> (Application)</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>HTTP routing by path, host or header; redirects; WebSockets; authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NLB</strong> (Network)</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>very fast, static IPs, any TCP/UDP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GWLB</strong> (Gateway)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>puts firewall appliances inline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classic</td>\n<td>4/7</td>\n<td>old: avoid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> an <strong>internet-facing ALB</strong> in the two public subnets.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A game server needs fixed IP addresses and plain TCP on a custom port. Which load balancer fits?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"ALB","ok":false},{"t":"NLB","ok":true},{"t":"Classic Load Balancer","ok":false},{"t":"CloudFront","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>NLB</strong> works at layer 4 and offers static IPs per AZ. The ALB is for HTTP(S).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m217--type-html-html-a-game-server-"}}]},{"title":"Inside an ALB","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Listeners, rules and target groups","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph ALB[\"Memos ALB\"]\n    L80[\"Listener :80 HTTP\"] -->|\"rule: redirect 301\"| L443\n    L443[\"Listener :443 HTTPS<br/>ACM cert · TLS 1.3/1.2\"] -->|\"default: forward\"| TG\n  end\n  TG[\"Target group memos-tg<br/>type ip · port 8081\"] --> Task[\"Fargate task IP<br/>10.0.3.x:8081\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li>A <strong>listener</strong> accepts a port and protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules</strong> (checked by priority) forward, redirect or return a fixed response.</li>\n<li>A <strong>target group</strong> is the set of backends plus its health-check settings.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fargate tasks register as <strong><code>target_type = \"ip\"</code></strong>, because each task has its own private IP (awsvpc networking mode).</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Health checks","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The ALB keeps requesting a <strong>path</strong> on every target:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Memos setting</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>path</td>\n<td><code>/</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>port</td>\n<td>8081</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>matcher</td>\n<td>200–399 counts as healthy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>interval</td>\n<td>30 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>healthy / unhealthy threshold</td>\n<td>2 / 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Improvement:</strong> Memos has a real <code>/healthz</code> endpoint, and the pipeline already checks it. Pointing the target group at <code>/healthz</code> too would make the ALB and CI agree on what \"healthy\" means.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"502, 503, 504 from an ALB","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Code</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>First thing to check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>502</strong> Bad Gateway</td>\n<td>the target gave a bad reply or reset the connection</td>\n<td>app crashing? wrong protocol or port?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>503</strong> Service Unavailable</td>\n<td><strong>no registered targets</strong> in the group</td>\n<td>is the ECS task running at all?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>504</strong> Gateway Timeout</td>\n<td>the target didn't reply in time</td>\n<td>slow app, or a security group blocking the ALB → task path</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ If <strong>every</strong> target is unhealthy, the ALB \"fails open\" and sends traffic to all of them anyway, so you usually see 502s, not 503s.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Right after a deploy the ALB returns <strong>503</strong>. What's the most likely situation?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The certificate expired","ok":false},{"t":"There are no registered targets: the new task isn't running or never registered","ok":true},{"t":"DNS is pointing to the wrong place","ok":false},{"t":"The app is slow","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>503 from an ALB means there's nothing in the target group to send to. Check the ECS service events and the task's stopped reason.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m218--type-html-html-right-after-a-"}}]},{"title":"Route 53 and DNS on AWS","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Hosted zones and records","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Route 53</strong> is AWS's DNS service. A <strong>hosted zone</strong> holds the records for a domain (<code>example.com</code>). The domain's registrar must point its <strong>NS records</strong> at the zone's four Route 53 name servers.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  REG[\"Registrar<br/>example.com NS →\"] --> HZ[\"Route 53 hosted zone<br/>example.com\"]\n  HZ --> A[\"A (alias) memos.example.com → ALB\"]\n  HZ --> V[\"CNAME _abc.memos.example.com → ACM validation\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Alias records","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An <strong>alias</strong> record is Route 53's special A/AAAA record that points at an AWS resource (ALB, CloudFront, S3 website…):\n- it follows the resource's <strong>changing IPs</strong> automatically\n- it works at the <strong>zone apex</strong> (<code>example.com</code>), where a CNAME isn't allowed\n- queries to AWS targets are <strong>free</strong></p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"resource \"aws_route53_record\" \"app\" {\n  zone_id = data.aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id\n  name    = \"memos.${var.domain_name}\"\n  type    = \"A\"\n  alias {\n    name                   = module.alb.dns_name\n    zone_id                = module.alb.alb_zone_id\n    evaluate_target_health = true\n  }\n}","file":"infra/main.tf"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Routing policies","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple</td>\n<td>one answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weighted</td>\n<td>e.g. 90/10 for a canary release</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latency</td>\n<td>send users to the fastest Region</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>primary, with a backup if its health check fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geolocation</td>\n<td>answer by the user's country, for data residency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why can't you use a CNAME record for <code>example.com</code> itself (the zone apex)?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"CNAMEs only work with IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"DNS rules forbid a CNAME at the apex, where NS and SOA records must exist. Route 53 alias records solve this.","ok":true},{"t":"Route 53 doesn't support CNAMEs","ok":false},{"t":"CNAMEs can't point to AWS resources","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The apex must hold NS and SOA records, and a CNAME can't coexist with other records. <strong>Alias</strong> gets around it.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m219--type-html-html-why-can-t-you-"}}]},{"title":"Certificates with ACM","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"ACM in one card","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>AWS Certificate Manager</strong> issues public TLS certificates <strong>for free</strong> and <strong>renews them automatically</strong>, for use on ALBs, CloudFront and API Gateway. The private key never leaves AWS.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Certificates are <strong>regional</strong> for ALBs (they must be in the ALB's Region). CloudFront needs them in <strong>us-east-1</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"DNS validation, automated with Terraform","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>ACM must check that you control the domain. With <strong>DNS validation</strong>, you create a special CNAME it gives you, and as long as that record exists, renewals happen automatically.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant TF as Terraform\n  participant ACM\n  participant R53 as Route 53\n  TF->>ACM: request a cert for example.com + memos.example.com\n  ACM-->>TF: please create CNAME _abc… → _xyz.acm-validations.aws\n  TF->>R53: create those validation records\n  ACM->>R53: looks up the CNAME\n  ACM-->>TF: ✅ ISSUED (aws_acm_certificate_validation waits for this)\n  TF->>TF: attach the cert to the HTTPS listener"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> the <code>acm</code> module uses a <code>for_each</code> over <code>domain_validation_options</code> to create every validation record, then <code>aws_acm_certificate_validation</code> blocks until the certificate is issued.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"CloudFront in one card","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>CloudFront</strong> is AWS's CDN: it caches content at edge locations near users. It speeds up static files, absorbs traffic spikes and works with <strong>WAF</strong> and <strong>Shield</strong> for protection. Memos doesn't use it yet, but putting CloudFront in front of the ALB is a common next step.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos use <strong>DNS</strong> validation for its ACM certificate instead of email validation?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Email validation costs money","ok":false},{"t":"It can be fully automated in Terraform, and it lets ACM renew the certificate automatically","ok":true},{"t":"Email validation doesn't work for subdomains","ok":false},{"t":"DNS validation is faster to type","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>As long as the validation CNAME stays in Route 53, ACM renews automatically, with no humans and no expiry surprises.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m220--type-html-html-why-does-memos"}}]}]},{"id":"ecs","title":"Containers on AWS: ECR, ECS & Fargate","level":"Intermediate","icon":"📦","summary":"Store images in ECR, run them with ECS on Fargate, the two IAM roles, deployments, rollbacks and how ECS compares with EKS.","topic":"AWS","lessons":[{"title":"ECR: the image registry","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What ECR is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Amazon ECR</strong> is a private Docker registry in your account. Images are pushed by CI and pulled by ECS.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  CI[GitHub Actions] -->|docker push memos:sha| ECR[(\"ECR repo: memos<br/>IMMUTABLE tags · scan on push\")]\n  ECR -->|pull| ECS[ECS task]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Tags: why the Git SHA, and why immutable","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Tag every image with the <strong>commit SHA</strong> (<code>memos:3f9c2e…</code>): unique, and traceable to the exact code.</li>\n<li><strong>IMMUTABLE</strong> tags mean a tag can never be overwritten, so <code>memos:&lt;sha&gt;</code> always means the same bytes. That's what makes <strong>rollback</strong> safe.</li>\n<li>Avoid deploying <code>latest</code>: you can't tell what's running.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> immutability broke pipeline re-runs at first (pushing the same tag twice is refused). The fix: <code>build.yml</code> checks ECR first and <strong>reuses</strong> the image if it already exists.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Scanning and lifecycle","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Scan on push</strong> checks images for known CVEs.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifecycle policies</strong> delete old images automatically (e.g. keep the last 30) to control cost.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why tag images with the Git commit SHA instead of <code>latest</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"SHAs are shorter","ok":false},{"t":"Each SHA is unique and maps to exact code, so you always know what's running and can roll back","ok":true},{"t":"ECR requires SHA tags","ok":false},{"t":"<code>latest</code> is slower to pull","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>latest</code> changes meaning every push. A SHA tag is a permanent, traceable version.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m221--type-html-html-why-tag-images"}}]},{"title":"ECS building blocks","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The four pieces","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  C[\"Cluster: memos-cluster<br/>(a logical group)\"] --> S[\"Service: memos-service<br/>keeps N copies running<br/>registers with the ALB\"]\n  S --> TD[\"Task definition: memos-task<br/>image · CPU · memory · port · roles · logs\"]\n  S --> T1[\"Task (running container)\"]\n  TD -.blueprint for.-> T1"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Task definition:</strong> the blueprint (like a docker-compose file). Each change creates a new <strong>revision</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Task:</strong> a running instance of a task definition.</li>\n<li><strong>Service:</strong> keeps the desired count of tasks running, replaces failures and does rolling deployments.</li>\n<li><strong>Cluster:</strong> groups services and tasks.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Fargate vs EC2 launch type","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fargate (Memos)</th>\n<th>EC2 launch type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Servers</td>\n<td>none to manage</td>\n<td>you run the instances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Billing</td>\n<td>per task vCPU/GB per second</td>\n<td>per instance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control</td>\n<td>less</td>\n<td>more (GPUs, daemons, special kernels)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Fargate CPU and memory come in fixed pairs: e.g. <strong>256 CPU (0.25 vCPU) with 512 MB to 2 GB</strong>. Memos uses 256 / 512.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"awsvpc networking","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>On Fargate every task gets <strong>its own network interface and private IP</strong> in your subnet, with <strong>its own security group</strong>. That's why the target group uses <code>target_type = \"ip\"</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does an ECS <strong>service</strong> do that a single task doesn't?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Builds the Docker image","ok":false},{"t":"Keeps the desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and registers them with the load balancer","ok":true},{"t":"Stores the image","ok":false},{"t":"Defines CPU and memory","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>service</strong> is the controller. The <strong>task definition</strong> is the blueprint, and a <strong>task</strong> is one running copy.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m222--type-html-html-what-does-an-e"}}]},{"title":"Two roles, two jobs","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Execution role vs task role","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>This is one of the most common ECS interview questions.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph ECS[\"ECS agent (before your app starts)\"]\n    ER[\"Execution role<br/>pull image from ECR<br/>write logs to CloudWatch<br/>fetch secrets\"]\n  end\n  subgraph APP[\"Your app (while it runs)\"]\n    TR[\"Task role<br/>whatever AWS APIs<br/>YOUR CODE calls<br/>(S3, SQS, …)\"]\n  end\n  ER --> APP"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Execution role</th>\n<th>Task role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Used by</td>\n<td>ECS itself</td>\n<td>your application code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memos policy</td>\n<td><code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code></td>\n<td><strong>none</strong>: Memos doesn't call AWS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> \"can't pull the image or read the secret\" → <strong>execution role</strong>. \"My app gets AccessDenied from S3\" → <strong>task role</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Injecting secrets","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Never put secrets in the image or in plain environment variables:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"secrets = [{\n  name      = \"MEMOS_DSN\"\n  valueFrom = aws_secretsmanager_secret.dsn.arn\n}]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>ECS fetches the value <strong>at start-up</strong> using the <strong>execution role</strong> (which needs <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code>) and injects it as an environment variable.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A new task fails with <strong>CannotPullContainerError</strong>, and you suspect permissions. Which role do you check?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The execution role","ok":true},{"t":"The task role","ok":false},{"t":"The GitHub Actions OIDC role","ok":false},{"t":"The ALB's role","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Pulling images, writing logs and fetching secrets all happen <strong>before</strong> your code runs, so they're done by ECS using the <strong>execution role</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m223--type-html-html-a-new-task-fai"}}]},{"title":"Deployments and rollbacks","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Rolling deployment","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When the task definition changes (for example, a new image tag), the service replaces old tasks with new ones:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant S as ECS service\n  participant N as New task (v2)\n  participant ALB\n  participant O as Old task (v1)\n  S->>N: start v2\n  N-->>ALB: register, then health checks pass\n  ALB->>N: send traffic\n  S->>O: drain connections, then stop v1"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>minimum_healthy_percent</code> and <code>maximum_percent</code> control how many tasks can be down or extra during the swap.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Deployment circuit breaker","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>If new tasks keep failing to become healthy, the <strong>circuit breaker</strong> stops the deployment, and with <code>rollback = true</code> it returns to the last working version automatically.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"deployment_circuit_breaker {\n  enable   = true\n  rollback = true\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Memos improvement:</strong> not enabled yet. Today, rollback is manual: rerun <code>deploy.yml</code> with the previous SHA.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Blue/green and canary","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Blue/green:</strong> run the new version alongside the old one, then switch all traffic at once (CodeDeploy can do this for ECS). Rollback is instant.</li>\n<li><strong>Canary:</strong> send a small percentage (say 10%) to the new version first, watch the metrics, then increase.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does enabling the ECS deployment circuit breaker with <code>rollback = true</code> give you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Faster image pulls","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic return to the last working deployment if new tasks can't become healthy","ok":true},{"t":"Blue/green deployments","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic scaling","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It detects a failing rollout and <strong>reverts by itself</strong>, with no human needed at 3 am.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m224--type-html-html-what-does-enab"}}]},{"title":"Scaling and the bigger picture","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"ECS service auto scaling","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Services scale <strong>out and in</strong> on metrics:\n- target tracking on <strong>CPU</strong> or <strong>memory</strong> (e.g. 60%)\n- target tracking on <strong>ALB requests per target</strong>\n- scheduled scaling</p>\n<p>Tasks spread across the subnets you give the service, so <strong>2+ tasks across 2 AZs</strong> gives you availability.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Memos can't scale out yet: two tasks would each have their <strong>own</strong> SQLite file. Fix the data tier first.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"ECS vs EKS","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>ECS</th>\n<th>EKS (Kubernetes)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning curve</td>\n<td>gentle</td>\n<td>steep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portability</td>\n<td>AWS only</td>\n<td>runs anywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem</td>\n<td>AWS integrations</td>\n<td>huge (Helm, operators, service meshes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control plane cost</td>\n<td>free</td>\n<td>about $0.10 per hour per cluster</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Pick <strong>ECS</strong> for simplicity on AWS. Pick <strong>EKS</strong> for portability, very large platforms, or if your team already knows Kubernetes.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Logs: awslogs","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Containers write to stdout and stderr. The <code>awslogs</code> driver ships those lines to <strong>CloudWatch Logs</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"logConfiguration = {\n  logDriver = \"awslogs\"\n  options = {\n    \"awslogs-group\"         = \"/ecs/memos\"\n    \"awslogs-region\"        = \"eu-west-1\"\n    \"awslogs-stream-prefix\" = \"ecs\"\n  }\n}"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why can't you just set <code>desired_count = 3</code> on Memos today to make it highly available?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Fargate only allows one task per service","ok":false},{"t":"Each task would have its own separate SQLite database, so users would see different data","ok":true},{"t":"The ALB can only route to one task","ok":false},{"t":"It would exceed the VPC's IP addresses","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>State lives <strong>inside</strong> each task. Move the data to RDS or EFS first, then scale out.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m225--type-html-html-why-can-t-you-"}}]}]},{"id":"terraform","title":"Terraform: infrastructure as code","level":"Intermediate","icon":"🏗️","summary":"HCL, the plan/apply workflow, state and locking, modules, variables, loops, importing and drift, using Memos' real Terraform layout.","topic":"Terraform","lessons":[{"title":"Infrastructure as code","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Why IaC?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Clicking in the console is slow, error-prone and leaves no record. <strong>Infrastructure as code (IaC)</strong> describes infrastructure in <strong>files</strong>, so it is:\n- <strong>repeatable:</strong> build identical dev, staging and prod environments\n- <strong>reviewable:</strong> changes go through pull requests\n- <strong>versioned:</strong> Git history of every change\n- <strong>automatable:</strong> pipelines apply it</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Declarative: describe the result","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Terraform is <strong>declarative</strong>: you describe <strong>what</strong> should exist, and Terraform works out <strong>how</strong> to get there.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  Code[\"Desired state<br/>(your .tf files)\"] --> TF{Terraform<br/>compares}\n  State[\"Known state<br/>(terraform.tfstate)\"] --> TF\n  Real[\"Real AWS<br/>(refreshed)\"] --> TF\n  TF --> Plan[\"Plan: + create · ~ update · - destroy\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Terraform vs the rest","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Terraform / OpenTofu</strong></td>\n<td>declarative IaC, any cloud</td>\n<td>huge provider ecosystem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CloudFormation</td>\n<td>declarative IaC, AWS only</td>\n<td>no state file to manage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CDK / Pulumi</td>\n<td>IaC in real programming languages</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ansible</td>\n<td>configuration management</td>\n<td>configures servers; mostly procedural</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does it mean that Terraform is <strong>declarative</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"You list every API call in order","ok":false},{"t":"You describe the end state and Terraform works out the steps","ok":true},{"t":"It only works on AWS","ok":false},{"t":"It runs on a schedule","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>You declare \"a VPC with this CIDR should exist\", and Terraform decides whether to create, update or leave it alone.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m226--type-html-html-what-does-it-m"}}]},{"title":"HCL and the workflow","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The building blocks","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"terraform {                          # settings + required providers + backend\n  required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" } }\n}\nprovider \"aws\" { region = var.aws_region }        # how to talk to AWS\n\nvariable \"vpc_cidr\" { type = string }             # input\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"main\" {                        # something to create\n  cidr_block = var.vpc_cidr\n}\ndata \"aws_route53_zone\" \"main\" { name = \"example.com\" }   # look up something that already exists\noutput \"vpc_id\" { value = aws_vpc.main.id }       # value to expose"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A reference such as <code>aws_vpc.main.id</code> creates a <strong>dependency</strong>, so Terraform builds things in the right order.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"init → plan → apply","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  W[write .tf] --> F[\"fmt / validate\"] --> I[\"init<br/>download providers<br/>connect the backend\"] --> P[\"plan<br/>preview changes\"] --> A[\"apply<br/>make them real\"]\n  A -.later.-> D[\"destroy<br/>remove everything\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform fmt</code></td>\n<td>format the code (<code>-check</code> in CI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform validate</code></td>\n<td>check syntax and references</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform init</code></td>\n<td>providers, modules, backend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code></td>\n<td>show <strong>and save</strong> the changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform apply tfplan</code></td>\n<td>apply exactly that plan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Reading a plan","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"  # aws_ecs_service.this will be updated in-place\n  ~ resource \"aws_ecs_service\" \"this\" {\n      ~ task_definition = \"memos-task:41\" -> (known after apply)\n    }\n  # aws_lb.this must be replaced\n-/+ resource \"aws_lb\" \"this\" { … }\n\nPlan: 1 to add, 1 to change, 1 to destroy."},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>+</code> create · <code>~</code> update in place · <code>-</code> destroy · <code>-/+</code> <strong>replace</strong> (destroy then create).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Always read <code>-/+</code> lines carefully. Replacing an ALB or a database is an outage.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos' pipeline run <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code> and then <code>terraform apply tfplan</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's faster than a plain apply","ok":false},{"t":"So exactly the reviewed plan is applied, with no surprise changes in between","ok":true},{"t":"<code>apply</code> can't run without a file","ok":false},{"t":"To skip the refresh","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A saved plan guarantees what you saw is what gets applied, even if something changed in the meantime.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m227--type-html-html-why-does-memos"}}]},{"title":"State and locking","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What state is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>terraform.tfstate</code> maps your code to <strong>real resource IDs</strong>, such as \"<code>aws_vpc.main</code> is <code>vpc-0abc…</code>\". Without it, Terraform can't tell what it already manages.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ State can contain <strong>secrets</strong> (database passwords, for example). Never commit it to Git.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Remote state + locking","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  Dev1[👩‍💻 you] -->|plan/apply| S3[(\"S3 bucket<br/>prod/terraform.tfstate<br/>versioned · encrypted\")]\n  CI[🤖 pipeline] -->|plan/apply| S3\n  S3 --- L[\"🔒 .tflock object<br/>(S3 native locking)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Remote</strong> state in S3 is shared by everyone and by CI, with <strong>versioning</strong> for recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Locking</strong> stops two applies running at once. Terraform 1.10+ supports <code>use_lockfile = true</code> on S3, so you no longer need a DynamoDB table.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"backend \"s3\" {\n  bucket       = \"memos-tfstate-example\"\n  key          = \"prod/terraform.tfstate\"\n  region       = \"eu-west-1\"\n  encrypt      = true\n  use_lockfile = true\n}","file":"infra/provider.tf"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The bootstrap pattern","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>There's a chicken-and-egg problem: the state bucket can't store its own state before it exists. So Memos uses <strong>two stacks</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph B[\"bootstrap/ (local state, run once)\"]\n    SB[S3 state bucket<br/>prevent_destroy]\n    OIDC[GitHub OIDC provider + role]\n  end\n  subgraph I[\"infra/ (remote state in that bucket)\"]\n    M[VPC · SG · ACM · ALB · ECR · IAM · ECS]\n  end\n  B -->|stores state for| I"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Long-lived things (state, CI identity) live separately from things you destroy and rebuild.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Drift and refresh","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Drift</strong> means reality no longer matches the code, for example when someone changes a security group in the console.\n- <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> shows drift <strong>without</strong> changing anything.\n- The next normal <code>apply</code> puts reality back to match the code.\n- <code>terraform refresh</code> is deprecated. Use <code>plan/apply -refresh-only</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Two engineers run <code>terraform apply</code> at the same moment against the Memos backend. What happens?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Both succeed and the state merges","ok":false},{"t":"The second one fails to get the state lock and changes nothing","ok":true},{"t":"The state file is corrupted","ok":false},{"t":"The newest apply wins","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>use_lockfile = true</code> creates a lock object. The second run gets \"Error acquiring the state lock\".</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m228--type-html-html-two-engineers-"}}]},{"title":"Modules, variables and loops","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Modules: reusable building blocks","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>module</strong> is a folder of <code>.tf</code> files with inputs (variables) and outputs. The root module calls child modules and wires them together:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  ROOT[infra/main.tf] --> VPC[modules/vpc]\n  ROOT --> SEC[modules/security]\n  ROOT --> ACM[modules/acm]\n  ROOT --> ALB[modules/alb]\n  ROOT --> ECR[modules/ecr]\n  ROOT --> IAM[modules/iam]\n  ROOT --> ECS[modules/ecs]\n  VPC -->|vpc_id, subnets| SEC & ALB & ECS\n  SEC -->|SG ids| ALB & ECS\n  ACM -->|certificate_arn| ALB\n  ALB -->|target_group_arn| ECS\n  IAM -->|role ARNs| ECS"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"module \"alb\" {\n  source          = \"./modules/alb\"\n  vpc_id          = module.vpc.vpc_id\n  public_subnets  = module.vpc.public_subnets\n  security_group  = module.security.alb_security_group_id\n  certificate_arn = module.acm.certificate_arn\n}","file":"infra/main.tf"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Variables, tfvars and outputs","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"variable \"environment\" {\n  type    = string\n  default = \"prod\"\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"dev\", \"prod\"], var.environment)\n    error_message = \"environment must be dev or prod.\"\n  }\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Values come from <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, <code>-var</code> flags or <code>TF_VAR_name</code> environment variables. Mark secrets <code>sensitive = true</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"count vs for_each","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"resource \"aws_subnet\" \"public\" {          # count: a list, indexed 0,1,2…\n  count      = length(var.public_subnet_cidrs)\n  cidr_block = var.public_subnet_cidrs[count.index]\n}\nresource \"aws_route53_record\" \"validation\" {   # for_each: a map, keyed by name\n  for_each = { for o in aws_acm_certificate.this.domain_validation_options : o.domain_name => o }\n  name     = each.value.resource_record_name\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ With <code>count</code>, removing a middle item <strong>shifts the indexes</strong> and can recreate resources. <code>for_each</code> keys are stable, so prefer it for anything you might remove.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Lifecycle rules","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>prevent_destroy = true</code></td>\n<td>refuse to destroy (Memos' state bucket)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>create_before_destroy = true</code></td>\n<td>build the replacement first (Memos' ACM cert)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ignore_changes = [desired_count]</code></td>\n<td>let autoscaling change it without Terraform reverting it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You manage subnets with <code>count</code> from a list of three CIDRs and delete the <strong>middle</strong> one. What could happen?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Only the middle subnet is destroyed","ok":false},{"t":"The third subnet's index shifts, so Terraform may destroy and recreate it","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing, because count is stable","ok":false},{"t":"Terraform refuses to plan","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>count</code> identifies resources by <strong>position</strong>. Use <code>for_each</code> with stable keys to avoid it.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m229--type-html-html-you-manage-sub"}}]},{"title":"Working safely","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Importing existing resources","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Things created by hand can be brought under Terraform with an <strong>import block</strong> (Terraform 1.5+):</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"import {\n  to = aws_route53_record.validation[\"example.com\"]\n  id = \"Z123…_abc.example.com_CNAME\"\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> moving ACM into code hit \"record already exists\". Import blocks adopted the existing records, and were removed after one apply.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Security scanning: tfsec","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>tfsec</strong> (now part of Trivy) scans Terraform for risky settings: public buckets, open security groups, missing encryption. Memos runs it on <strong>every</strong> pipeline run.</p>\n<p>If you accept a finding, document <strong>why</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"# Flow Logs are omitted to avoid extra logging costs.\n# tfsec:ignore:aws-ec2-require-vpc-flow-logs-for-all-vpcs\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"main\" { … }"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Environments and workspaces","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Keep environments <strong>isolated</strong>: separate state per environment (different backend keys, or separate folders or accounts) and separate tfvars. One bad apply should never touch two environments.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A security group rule was added by hand in the console. How do you see this drift <strong>without</strong> changing anything?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>terraform apply</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform destroy</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform fmt</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>-refresh-only</code> compares state with reality and reports differences without proposing changes to your resources.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m230--type-html-html-a-security-gro"}}]}]},{"id":"cicd","title":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","level":"Intermediate","icon":"🔁","summary":"CI vs CD, workflow anatomy, triggers, secrets, keyless AWS login with OIDC, and Memos' build → deploy → verify pipeline.","topic":"CI/CD","lessons":[{"title":"CI and CD","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Continuous integration","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>CI</strong> means everyone merges small changes to <code>main</code> often, and every push is <strong>automatically built and tested</strong>. Bugs show up in minutes, not at release time.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  P[push / PR] --> B[build] --> L[lint + format] --> T[unit tests] --> S[security scans] --> A[(artifact:<br/>image :sha)]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Continuous delivery vs deployment","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Continuous delivery:</strong> every change that passes is <strong>ready</strong> to release. A human presses the button.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous deployment:</strong> every change that passes <strong>goes to production automatically</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  CI[CI passes] --> STG[deploy to staging] --> GATE{\"approval?\"}\n  GATE -->|\"delivery: a human approves\"| PROD[production]\n  GATE -->|\"deployment: automatic\"| PROD"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>In Memos:</strong> continuous <strong>deployment</strong>. A merge to <code>main</code> that changes the app goes all the way to production.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Build once, promote everywhere","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Build the artefact (the image) <strong>once</strong>, give it an immutable tag, and deploy <strong>that same artefact</strong> to every environment. Rebuilding per environment risks shipping something you never tested.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delivery is for apps; deployment is for infrastructure","ok":false},{"t":"Delivery keeps every change releasable but needs a human to release; deployment releases automatically","ok":true},{"t":"There is no difference","ok":false},{"t":"Deployment skips testing","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Both automate build and test. The difference is whether production needs a <strong>manual approval</strong>.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m231--type-html-html-what-s-the-dif"}}]},{"title":"GitHub Actions anatomy","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Workflows, jobs, steps","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  WF[\"Workflow (.github/workflows/build.yml)\"] --> TR[\"on: triggers\"]\n  WF --> J1[\"Job: build<br/>runs-on: ubuntu-latest\"]\n  J1 --> S1[\"step: checkout\"] --> S2[\"step: tfsec\"] --> S3[\"step: AWS login (OIDC)\"] --> S4[\"step: docker build + push\"]\n  WF --> J2[\"Job: health-check<br/>needs: deploy\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Workflow:</strong> a YAML file. <strong>Triggers</strong> (<code>on:</code>) start it.</li>\n<li><strong>Jobs</strong> run on fresh <strong>runners</strong> (VMs), in parallel unless they declare <code>needs:</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Steps</strong> run in order: a shell command (<code>run:</code>) or a reusable <strong>action</strong> (<code>uses:</code>).</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Triggers","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    paths: ['app/**', 'Dockerfile', '.dockerignore']   # only when the app changes\n  pull_request:\n  workflow_dispatch:          # a manual \"Run workflow\" button, with inputs\n  workflow_run:               # after another workflow finishes\n    workflows: [\"Build and Push\"]\n    types: [completed]\n  schedule:\n    - cron: \"0 3 * * 1\"       # Mondays 03:00 UTC"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Secrets and variables","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>${{ secrets.NAME }}</code> for sensitive values. They're <strong>masked</strong> in logs, and not given to workflows triggered by pull requests from forks.</li>\n<li><code>${{ vars.NAME }}</code> for plain settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Environments</strong> (e.g. <code>production</code>) can hold their own secrets and <strong>require reviewers</strong> before a job runs.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Third-party actions run code with your permissions. Pin them to a <strong>full commit SHA</strong>, not just a tag, because tags can be moved.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In GitHub Actions, how do you make a job wait for another job to finish?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Put them in the same step","ok":false},{"t":"Use <code>needs: &lt;job-id&gt;</code>","ok":true},{"t":"Use <code>on: workflow_run</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Jobs always run in order","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Jobs run <strong>in parallel</strong> by default. <code>needs:</code> creates the dependency (<code>health-check</code> needs <code>deploy</code> in Memos).</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m232--type-html-html-in-github-acti"}}]},{"title":"Keyless AWS login with OIDC","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The problem with access keys","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Storing <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code>/<code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> in GitHub means long-lived credentials that can leak, need rotating and work from anywhere.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"How OIDC works","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant GH as GitHub Actions job\n  participant IdP as GitHub OIDC provider\n  participant STS as AWS STS\n  participant AWS as AWS APIs\n  GH->>IdP: request an ID token (needs permissions id-token: write)\n  IdP-->>GH: signed JWT: sub=repo:your-username/memos-aws-infra:ref:refs/heads/main, aud=sts.amazonaws.com\n  GH->>STS: AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity(role ARN + JWT)\n  STS->>STS: verify signature + check the role's trust policy conditions\n  STS-->>GH: temporary credentials (expire, ~1 hour)\n  GH->>AWS: push to ECR, terraform apply…"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>No secret is stored. The only GitHub secret is the <strong>role ARN</strong>, which isn't a credential.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The trust policy is everything","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"Condition = {\n  StringEquals = { \"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud\" = \"sts.amazonaws.com\" }\n  StringLike   = { \"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub\" = \"repo:your-username/memos-aws-infra:*\" }\n}","file":"bootstrap/oidc.tf"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>aud</code> must be <code>sts.amazonaws.com</code>.</li>\n<li><code>sub</code> decides <strong>which repo, branch or environment</strong> may assume the role.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Classic failure:</strong> OIDC keeps failing because the <code>sub</code> doesn't match, for example after a repo is renamed. The fix is rebuilding the provider and role with a correct trust policy, then moving it into <code>bootstrap/</code>.</p>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>Tighten it:</strong> <code>:*</code> allows any branch. <code>repo:…:ref:refs/heads/main</code> or <code>repo:…:environment:production</code> is safer. The role also has AdministratorAccess today, which is too broad.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The OIDC step fails with \"Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\". What do you check first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Docker image tag","ok":false},{"t":"The role's trust policy: do the <code>sub</code> and <code>aud</code> conditions match the token GitHub sent?","ok":true},{"t":"The ECS service events","ok":false},{"t":"The S3 bucket policy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Almost every OIDC failure is a <strong>trust-policy mismatch</strong> (wrong repo name, branch, environment or audience), or a missing <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m233--type-html-html-the-oidc-step-"}}]},{"title":"The Memos pipeline","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Three workflows","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph B[\"build.yml · push to main (app/**, Dockerfile)\"]\n    b1[checkout] --> b2[tfsec] --> b3[OIDC login] --> b4{\"image :sha<br/>in ECR?\"}\n    b4 -->|yes| b5[reuse it]\n    b4 -->|no| b6[\"docker build + push\"]\n  end\n  subgraph D[\"deploy.yml · after build succeeds, or manual\"]\n    d1[\"validate SHA + image exists\"] --> d2[\"fmt -check · init · validate\"] --> d3[\"plan -out=tfplan\"] --> d4[apply tfplan] --> d5[\"wait 60s · curl /healthz\"]\n  end\n  subgraph X[\"destroy.yml · manual only\"]\n    x1[terraform destroy]\n  end\n  B --> D"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Rolling back","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every image stays in ECR under its SHA. To roll back, run <strong>Deploy</strong> manually with the previous good SHA:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  BAD[\"v2 deployed 💥\"] --> RUN[\"Actions → Deploy → Run workflow<br/>image_tag = &lt;v1 sha&gt;\"] --> TF[\"terraform apply<br/>image_url = …:v1\"] --> OK[\"ECS rolls back to v1 ✅\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Improve:</strong> add the ECS circuit breaker for automatic rollback, and a <code>terraform plan</code> on every pull request, posted as a comment for review.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Gotcha: path filters","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>build.yml</code> only triggers on <code>app/**</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code> and <code>.dockerignore</code>. A change <strong>only to <code>infra/**</code></strong> doesn't trigger a build, so the deploy never runs. You'd run <strong>Deploy</strong> manually, or add an infra-specific trigger.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Deployment strategies","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Rollback</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Recreate</td>\n<td>stop old, start new</td>\n<td>slow, with downtime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rolling</strong> (Memos)</td>\n<td>replace tasks gradually</td>\n<td>redeploy the old version</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blue/green</td>\n<td>full new copy, then switch traffic</td>\n<td>flip back instantly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canary</td>\n<td>small % first, then more</td>\n<td>stop the canary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You change only <code>infra/modules/alb/main.tf</code> and merge to main. Why doesn't anything deploy?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"tfsec blocked it","ok":false},{"t":"build.yml's path filter only matches app/**, Dockerfile and .dockerignore, so the build (and the deploy that follows it) never starts","ok":true},{"t":"Terraform changes need a pull request","ok":false},{"t":"The OIDC role can't change ALBs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> runs <strong>after</strong> <code>build.yml</code>. No build means no deploy, so trigger it manually or add an <code>infra/**</code> trigger.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m234--type-html-html-you-change-onl"}}]}]},{"id":"observability","title":"Monitoring, logs & troubleshooting","level":"Advanced","icon":"🔭","summary":"Logs, metrics and traces, CloudWatch and alarms, SLOs, a fault-finding method and the playbooks for Memos' most likely failures.","topic":"Troubleshooting","lessons":[{"title":"Observability basics","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Monitoring vs observability","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> tells you <strong>that</strong> something is wrong: a metric crossed a line, an alarm fired.</li>\n<li><strong>Observability</strong> lets you work out <strong>why</strong>, including problems you never predicted, by asking new questions of the data.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The three pillars","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  L[\"📜 Logs<br/>individual events<br/>'user 42 login failed'\"]\n  M[\"📈 Metrics<br/>numbers over time<br/>CPU %, requests/s, p95 latency\"]\n  T[\"🧵 Traces<br/>one request's path<br/>ALB → app → DB\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pillar</th>\n<th>Answers</th>\n<th>AWS tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Logs</td>\n<td>what exactly happened?</td>\n<td>CloudWatch Logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>how much and how fast?</td>\n<td>CloudWatch Metrics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traces</td>\n<td>where is the time going?</td>\n<td>X-Ray / OpenTelemetry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The golden signals","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>For any service, watch the <strong>four golden signals</strong>:\n1. <strong>Latency:</strong> how long requests take (watch p95/p99, not just the average)\n2. <strong>Traffic:</strong> how many requests\n3. <strong>Errors:</strong> the rate of failures (5xx)\n4. <strong>Saturation:</strong> how full it is (CPU, memory, connections)</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which pillar of observability shows the path of a <strong>single request</strong> through several services?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Logs","ok":false},{"t":"Metrics","ok":false},{"t":"Traces","ok":true},{"t":"Alarms","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>trace</strong> follows one request across hops, with timings for each, and shows where it's slow.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m235--type-html-html-which-pillar-o"}}]},{"title":"CloudWatch and alarms","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"CloudWatch in Memos","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  T[\"Memos task<br/>stdout / stderr\"] -->|awslogs driver| LG[\"Log group /ecs/memos<br/>retention 7 days\"]\n  ECS[ECS service] --> MET[\"Metrics: CPU, memory\"]\n  ALB[ALB] --> MET2[\"Metrics: 5xx, latency,<br/>healthy host count\"]\n  MET & MET2 --> AL{Alarm} --> SNS[SNS → email / Slack]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Memos trade-offs:</strong> 7-day log retention and <strong>Container Insights off</strong> (both to save cost). There are no alarms yet. Adding one on ALB 5xx and <code>UnHealthyHostCount</code> is a quick win.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Logs Insights","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Query logs like a database:</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /error/\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 20"}]},{"type":"card","title":"SLIs, SLOs and error budgets","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>SLI</strong> (indicator): what you measure, such as \"% of requests that succeed in under 500 ms\".</li>\n<li><strong>SLO</strong> (objective): the target, such as \"99.9% over 30 days\".</li>\n<li><strong>Error budget:</strong> what's left over (0.1% ≈ <strong>43 minutes</strong> a month). Plenty left means ship faster. Used up means slow down and fix reliability.</li>\n<li><strong>SLA:</strong> a contract with customers, usually looser than your SLO.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An SLO is 99.9% availability over 30 days. Roughly how much downtime does the error budget allow?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"4 minutes","ok":false},{"t":"43 minutes","ok":true},{"t":"7 hours","ok":false},{"t":"3 days","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>30 days × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that ≈ <strong>43 minutes</strong>.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m236--type-html-html-an-slo-is-99-9"}}]},{"title":"A method for troubleshooting","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Layer by layer, one change at a time","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  A[\"1. What changed? (deploy, config, traffic)\"] --> B[\"2. Scope: everyone or some users? which endpoint?\"]\n  B --> C[\"3. Work through the path, outside in:<br/>DNS → ALB → target group → ECS task → app logs → dependencies\"]\n  C --> D[\"4. Form ONE hypothesis, test it, change one thing\"]\n  D --> E[\"5. Fix → verify → write it up (blameless post-mortem)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>Errors right after a release? Roll back first</strong> and investigate after. Recovery beats root cause during an incident.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Where to look, in order","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DNS</td>\n<td><code>dig memos.example.com</code>: right ALB?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ALB</td>\n<td>listener and certificate OK? status code: 502/503/504?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target group</td>\n<td>healthy targets? health-check failure reason?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ECS</td>\n<td>service <strong>events</strong>, task <strong>stopped reason</strong>, exit code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App</td>\n<td>CloudWatch Logs <code>/ecs/memos</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network</td>\n<td>security group chain ALB SG → ECS SG on 8081, NAT route for pulls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Blameless post-mortems","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>After an incident write: <strong>timeline · impact · root cause · what went well · action items</strong>. Blameless means you fix <strong>systems</strong>, not people. People hide mistakes when they fear blame.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Users report errors two minutes after a new deploy. What's the best <strong>first</strong> action?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Read through the code changes line by line","ok":false},{"t":"Roll back to the previous version, then investigate","ok":true},{"t":"Restart the database","ok":false},{"t":"Wait to see if it fixes itself","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Restore service first. With immutable SHA tags, rolling back is fast and safe. Debug afterwards.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m237--type-html-html-users-report-e"}}]},{"title":"Memos playbooks","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"502 / 503 / unreachable","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Target group:</strong> any healthy targets? (503 means none registered)</li>\n<li><strong>ECS:</strong> is the task running, or stopping in a loop? Read the stopped reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Port:</strong> 8081 <strong>everywhere</strong>: <code>MEMOS_PORT</code>, the task definition, the target group, both SG rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Health check:</strong> does <code>/</code> (or <code>/healthz</code>) return 200–399?</li>\n<li><strong>Security groups:</strong> ECS SG allows 8081 <strong>from the ALB SG</strong>, and the ALB SG has <strong>egress</strong> to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Logs:</strong> <code>/ecs/memos</code>: crashing on start?</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Classic cause:</strong> port drift between 5230 (Memos' default) and 8081 makes targets unhealthy. The lesson: define the port <strong>once</strong> as a variable.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The task won't start","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  S[Task STOPPED] --> R{stopped reason}\n  R -->|CannotPullContainerError| P[\"image tag exists?<br/>execution role ECR permissions?<br/>NAT route / VPC endpoints?<br/>SG allows 443 out?\"]\n  R -->|\"Essential container exited (code 1)\"| L[\"read logs: bad config, missing env var\"]\n  R -->|\"OutOfMemoryError / exit 137\"| M[\"raise memory (512 MB today), find the leak\"]\n  R -->|\"ResourceInitializationError (secrets)\"| SE[\"execution role can't read the secret,<br/>or no route to Secrets Manager\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A timeout or hang","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>timeout</strong> means traffic is being <strong>dropped</strong>: check security groups, NACLs and route tables. <strong>Connection refused</strong> is different: the host was reached, but nothing is listening.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A task stops with <code>CannotPullContainerError</code>. It runs in a private subnet. Which is <strong>not</strong> a likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The private route table has no route to a NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"The execution role lacks ECR permissions","ok":false},{"t":"The image tag doesn't exist in ECR","ok":false},{"t":"The ALB's health check path is wrong","ok":true}],"explain":"<p>Pulling happens <strong>before</strong> the app starts, so health checks don't matter yet. Routing, IAM and the tag itself do.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m238--type-html-html-a-task-stops-w"}}]}]},{"id":"security-reliability","title":"Security, reliability & cost","level":"Advanced","icon":"🛡️","summary":"Least privilege, secrets, defence in depth, HA vs DR, RPO and RTO, the Well-Architected pillars and where cloud money goes.","topic":"Security","lessons":[{"title":"Security in layers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Defence in depth","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>No single control is perfect, so you stack them. An attacker has to beat <strong>every</strong> layer.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  I((Internet)) --> E[\"Edge<br/>WAF · Shield · HTTPS only\"]\n  E --> N[\"Network<br/>private subnets · SG-to-SG rules\"]\n  N --> C[\"Compute<br/>non-root · minimal image · scanned\"]\n  C --> ID[\"Identity<br/>least-privilege roles · no long-lived keys\"]\n  ID --> D[\"Data<br/>encryption at rest + in transit · backups\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Memos' security, honestly","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Already good</th>\n<th>⚠️ To improve</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tasks in private subnets</td>\n<td>OIDC role has AdministratorAccess</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SG-to-SG rule on 8081 only</td>\n<td>trust policy <code>sub</code> is <code>repo:…:*</code> (any branch)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HTTPS only (301 redirect, TLS 1.2+)</td>\n<td>actions pinned by tag, not SHA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OIDC: no stored keys</td>\n<td>no WAF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-root container, scan on push</td>\n<td>no flow logs (accepted cost trade-off)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State bucket: versioned, encrypted, HTTPS-only</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Secrets management","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Good for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Secrets Manager</strong></td>\n<td>database passwords and API keys, with <strong>automatic rotation</strong>; small monthly fee per secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SSM Parameter Store</strong> (SecureString)</td>\n<td>config and secrets; the standard tier is free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KMS</strong></td>\n<td>the encryption keys behind both, plus EBS, S3 and RDS encryption</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Never put secrets in Git, Docker images, Terraform variables in plain text, or logs.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which AWS service can <strong>automatically rotate</strong> a database password?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"KMS","ok":false},{"t":"Secrets Manager","ok":true},{"t":"IAM","ok":false},{"t":"S3","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Secrets Manager has built-in rotation (for RDS and others). Parameter Store stores secrets but doesn't rotate them for you.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m239--type-html-html-which-aws-serv"}}]},{"title":"Detect and audit","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Who did what: CloudTrail","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>CloudTrail</strong> records <strong>API calls</strong> in the account: who, what, when and from where. It's the first place to look for \"who deleted the security group?\"</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CloudTrail</strong></td>\n<td>audit log of API activity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GuardDuty</strong></td>\n<td>threat detection from logs (crypto-mining, odd logins)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security Hub</strong></td>\n<td>central findings and best-practice checks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inspector</strong></td>\n<td>vulnerability scanning for EC2, ECR and Lambda</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Config</strong></td>\n<td>history of resource configuration and compliance rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WAF / Shield</strong></td>\n<td>block bad HTTP traffic / DDoS protection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Supply-chain security","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Your pipeline runs other people's code: actions, base images, packages.\n- Pin actions to <strong>commit SHAs</strong>. Pin base image versions.\n- <strong>Scan</strong> images and dependencies (Trivy, ECR scanning, Dependabot).\n- Give CI only the permissions it needs (<code>permissions:</code> in workflows, least-privilege roles).</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Someone deleted a security group last night. Which service tells you who did it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"CloudWatch Metrics","ok":false},{"t":"CloudTrail","ok":true},{"t":"GuardDuty","ok":false},{"t":"Inspector","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>CloudTrail logs every API call, including <code>DeleteSecurityGroup</code>, with the identity that made it.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m240--type-html-html-someone-delete"}}]},{"title":"Reliability","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"High availability vs fault tolerance vs DR","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>High availability (HA):</strong> keeps working with at most a short blip (multi-AZ, several tasks, health checks).</li>\n<li><strong>Fault tolerance:</strong> <strong>no</strong> interruption at all (fully redundant, more expensive).</li>\n<li><strong>Disaster recovery (DR):</strong> how you recover from losing a whole Region or a whole system.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"RPO and RTO","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  B[\"last good backup\"] -- \"RPO: how much DATA you can lose\" --> X((\"💥 disaster\"))\n  X -- \"RTO: how long you can be DOWN\" --> R[\"service restored\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>RPO</strong> (Recovery Point Objective): the most data loss you can accept, measured in time.</li>\n<li><strong>RTO</strong> (Recovery Time Objective): the most downtime you can accept.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"DR strategies, cheapest to most expensive","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[\"Backup & restore<br/>hours · $\"] --> B[\"Pilot light<br/>core data replicated<br/>tens of minutes · $$\"] --> C[\"Warm standby<br/>scaled-down copy running<br/>minutes · $$$\"] --> D[\"Multi-site active/active<br/>~zero · $$$$\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The lower the RPO and RTO, the higher the cost. Match the strategy to what the business actually needs.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Memos keeps its SQLite data inside the container with no backups. What are its RPO and RTO for data if a task is replaced?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"RPO zero, RTO zero","ok":false},{"t":"The data is simply lost: there's no recovery point at all","ok":true},{"t":"RPO 24 hours, RTO 1 hour","ok":false},{"t":"RPO 7 days, because of log retention","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>With no backup and ephemeral storage, there's nothing to restore. That's why moving to RDS (with backups) or EFS is the top improvement.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m241--type-html-html-memos-keeps-it"}}]},{"title":"Well-Architected and cost","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The six pillars","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <strong>AWS Well-Architected Framework</strong> reviews a design against six pillars:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  WA((Well-Architected)) --> OE[Operational excellence]\n  WA --> SE[Security]\n  WA --> RE[Reliability]\n  WA --> PE[Performance efficiency]\n  WA --> CO[Cost optimisation]\n  WA --> SU[Sustainability]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Where cloud money goes","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Driver</th>\n<th>Memos example</th>\n<th>Ways to save</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Always-on networking</td>\n<td><strong>2 NAT gateways</strong>, ALB, public IPv4</td>\n<td>one NAT in non-prod, VPC endpoints, destroy when idle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compute</td>\n<td>Fargate 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB</td>\n<td>right-size, Fargate Spot for non-prod, Savings Plans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data transfer</td>\n<td>traffic through the NAT</td>\n<td>S3 gateway endpoint (free)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage and logs</td>\n<td>7-day log retention</td>\n<td>lifecycle rules, shorter retention</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Habit:</strong> tag everything (<code>Project = \"Memos\"</code>, <code>ManagedBy = \"Terraform\"</code> via <code>default_tags</code>), turn on <strong>Cost Explorer</strong> and set <strong>AWS Budgets</strong> alerts.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which is a <strong>free</strong> way to stop Memos' ECR image layer downloads going through the NAT gateway?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An interface endpoint for ECR","ok":false},{"t":"An S3 gateway endpoint (image layers are stored in S3)","ok":true},{"t":"A second NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"CloudFront","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Gateway endpoints for S3 cost nothing. You still need the ECR API endpoints (paid) or the NAT for the small API calls.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m242--type-html-html-which-is-a-fre"}}]}]},{"id":"k8s-core","title":"Kubernetes 1: the core ideas","level":"Advanced","icon":"☸️","summary":"Why orchestration exists, how a cluster is built, a free practice cluster with kind, YAML, pods, labels and namespaces.","topic":"Kubernetes","lessons":[{"title":"Why Kubernetes","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The problem it solves","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>One container is easy to run. Hundreds, across many machines, all needing to be <strong>started, watched, restarted, updated, scaled and connected</strong>, is not.</p>\n<p>A <strong>container orchestrator</strong> does that boring work for you:\n- <strong>Scheduling:</strong> which machine runs what\n- <strong>Scaling:</strong> more or fewer copies\n- <strong>Self-healing:</strong> restart what crashes, move work off dead machines\n- <strong>Rolling updates:</strong> new versions with no downtime\n- <strong>Load balancing</strong>, <strong>storage</strong> and <strong>secrets</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes</strong> (K8s: K, 8 letters, s) is the industry-standard orchestrator. Google built it, and the CNCF looks after it now.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The single most important idea: desired state","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You don't tell Kubernetes <strong>how</strong>. You tell it <strong>what you want</strong>, and it keeps making that true, forever.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  Y[\"📄 You declare<br/>'3 copies of game:v2'\"] --> K{\"Kubernetes<br/>compares\"}\n  R[\"🔍 Reality<br/>2 running\"] --> K\n  K -->|\"difference → fix it\"| A[\"start 1 more pod\"]\n  A --> R"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>If a container crashes, a node dies or someone deletes a pod, reality no longer matches what you asked for, so Kubernetes fixes it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 Kubernetes is Linux, networking and containers, scaled up across a fleet of machines and managed from one control centre. If you know those three, you're halfway there.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The building blocks","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  ING[\"Ingress<br/>lets the internet in\"] --> SVC[\"Service<br/>stable address\"]\n  SVC --> P1[Pod] & P2[Pod] & P3[Pod]\n  DEP[\"Deployment<br/>keeps N pods running\"] -.manages.-> P1 & P2 & P3\n  CM[\"ConfigMap / Secret<br/>settings + passwords\"] -.-> P1\n  PVC[\"PersistentVolumeClaim<br/>data that survives\"] -.-> P1"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Everything is described in <strong>YAML</strong>, and objects find each other with <strong>labels</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Kubernetes vs the ECS example","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>If you did the ECS Lab, most ideas map straight across:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>ECS (the Memos example)</th>\n<th>Kubernetes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Group of machines</td>\n<td>ECS cluster</td>\n<td>Cluster (EKS on AWS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recipe for the app</td>\n<td>Task definition</td>\n<td>Pod spec in a Deployment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One running copy</td>\n<td>Task</td>\n<td>Pod</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep N copies, roll out updates</td>\n<td>ECS service (<code>desired_count</code>)</td>\n<td>Deployment (<code>replicas</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stable address</td>\n<td>ALB target group</td>\n<td>Service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HTTPS + routing by host</td>\n<td>ALB listener</td>\n<td>Ingress + ingress controller</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certificate</td>\n<td>ACM</td>\n<td>cert-manager + Let's Encrypt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DNS record</td>\n<td>Route 53 via Terraform</td>\n<td>ExternalDNS (automatic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Settings / passwords</td>\n<td>env vars / Secrets Manager</td>\n<td>ConfigMap / Secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Firewall between parts</td>\n<td>Security groups</td>\n<td>NetworkPolicy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App's AWS permissions</td>\n<td>Task role</td>\n<td>Service account + IRSA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Interview answer, \"ECS or Kubernetes?\":</strong> ECS is simpler and AWS-only. Kubernetes is the open-source standard: it runs on any cloud and has a huge ecosystem (Helm, Argo CD, cert-manager), but there's more to learn and operate.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does \"desired state\" mean in Kubernetes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The state you hope the app reaches after testing","ok":false},{"t":"What you declare should be running; Kubernetes keeps working to make reality match it","ok":true},{"t":"The last state saved in a backup","ok":false},{"t":"The state of the control plane only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Declare it once, and control loops keep fixing any difference: that's self-healing.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m243--type-html-html-what-does-desi"}}]},{"title":"Cluster architecture","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Control plane and worker nodes","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  subgraph CP[\"Control plane: the brain (WHAT and WHERE)\"]\n    API[\"kube-apiserver<br/>front door\"]\n    ETCD[(\"etcd<br/>the memory\")]\n    SCH[\"kube-scheduler<br/>picks a node\"]\n    CM[\"controller-manager<br/>fixes differences\"]\n    API --- ETCD\n    API --- SCH\n    API --- CM\n  end\n  subgraph W1[\"Worker node: the muscle (HOW)\"]\n    KL1[kubelet] --> RT1[containerd] --> P1[\"pods\"]\n    KP1[kube-proxy]\n  end\n  subgraph W2[\"Worker node\"]\n    KL2[kubelet] --> RT2[containerd] --> P2[\"pods\"]\n    KP2[kube-proxy]\n  end\n  API --> KL1 & KL2"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The restaurant analogy","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>API server:</strong> the order desk. Every order goes through it, and nobody walks into the kitchen.</li>\n<li><strong>etcd:</strong> the order book, the official record of everything.</li>\n<li><strong>Scheduler:</strong> decides which chef gets each new order.</li>\n<li><strong>Controller-manager:</strong> keeps checking \"did every table get what it ordered?\"</li>\n<li><strong>Worker nodes:</strong> kitchen stations. The <strong>kubelet</strong> is the station chef, and <strong>kube-proxy</strong> is the waiter who knows which station serves which table.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 You only ever talk to the <strong>API server</strong>. It saves your desired state in etcd, and the other components make it happen.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"What each part does","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>kube-apiserver</td>\n<td>control plane</td>\n<td>every request goes through it; validates and stores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>etcd</td>\n<td>control plane</td>\n<td>key-value store holding all cluster state (back it up!)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>kube-scheduler</td>\n<td>control plane</td>\n<td>chooses a node for each new pod (free CPU and memory, rules)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>controller-manager</td>\n<td>control plane</td>\n<td>reconcile loops: actual → desired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cloud-controller-manager</td>\n<td>control plane (cloud only)</td>\n<td>creates cloud load balancers and disks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>kubelet</td>\n<td>every node</td>\n<td>starts and watches that node's containers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>kube-proxy</td>\n<td>every node</td>\n<td>network rules so Services reach the right pods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>container runtime</td>\n<td>every node</td>\n<td>actually runs containers (containerd, CRI-O)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"What happens when you apply something","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant You as kubectl\n  participant API as API server\n  participant E as etcd\n  participant C as controllers\n  participant S as scheduler\n  participant K as kubelet (node)\n  You->>API: apply deploy.yaml (3 replicas)\n  API->>E: save desired state\n  C->>API: 3 wanted, 0 exist → create 3 pod records\n  S->>API: assign each pod to a node\n  K->>API: I have a new pod\n  K->>K: pull image, start container"}]},{"type":"card","title":"On EKS, who runs what?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>On <strong>EKS</strong>, AWS runs and scales the <strong>whole control plane</strong> across three AZs. You manage the <strong>worker nodes</strong> (node groups) and what runs on them. ECS hides all of this from you.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which component do <code>kubectl</code> commands actually talk to?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"etcd","ok":false},{"t":"The kubelet on each node","ok":false},{"t":"The kube-apiserver","ok":true},{"t":"The scheduler","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Everything goes through the API server, which stores the desired state in etcd.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m244--type-html-html-which-componen"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which component decides <strong>which node</strong> a new pod runs on?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"kubelet","ok":false},{"t":"kube-scheduler","ok":true},{"t":"kube-proxy","ok":false},{"t":"etcd","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The scheduler matches pods to nodes with enough free resources. The kubelet on that node then starts the containers.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m245--type-html-html-which-componen"}}]},{"title":"A free practice cluster with kind","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"kind: Kubernetes in Docker","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Cloud clusters cost money. <strong>kind</strong> runs each \"node\" as a Docker container, so you get a <strong>real multi-node cluster on your laptop, for free</strong>. (Alternatives: minikube, k3d, Docker Desktop's built-in Kubernetes.)</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"kind: Cluster\napiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4\nnodes:\n  - role: control-plane\n  - role: worker\n  - role: worker","file":"kind-config.yaml"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  L[\"💻 your laptop\"] --> D[Docker]\n  D --> N1[\"container = control-plane node\"]\n  D --> N2[\"container = worker node\"]\n  D --> N3[\"container = worker node\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Create it and look around","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ kind create cluster --config kind-config.yaml --name k8s-demo\n$ kubectl get nodes\nNAME                     STATUS   ROLES           AGE\nk8s-demo-control-plane   Ready    control-plane   60s\nk8s-demo-worker          Ready    <none>          40s\nk8s-demo-worker2         Ready    <none>          40s"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>kubectl config get-contexts</code> lists every cluster you can talk to. kind names its context <code>kind-&lt;name&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> switches cluster.</li>\n<li><code>kind delete cluster --name k8s-demo</code> removes it.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"kubeconfig and contexts","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>kubectl</code> reads <strong><code>~/.kube/config</code></strong> to know which clusters exist and how to log in. Each <strong>context</strong> is one cluster plus one user.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  K[kubectl] --> CFG[\"~/.kube/config\"]\n  CFG --> C1[\"context: kind-k8s-demo\"]\n  CFG --> C2[\"context: arn:aws:eks:…:cluster/eks-lab\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Always check the context before destructive commands: <code>kubectl config current-context</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do you need installed to run a kind cluster?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An AWS account","ok":false},{"t":"Docker (running) and kubectl","ok":true},{"t":"A VirtualBox VM per node","ok":false},{"t":"Helm","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>kind's nodes <strong>are</strong> Docker containers. kubectl is how you talk to the cluster.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m246--type-html-html-what-do-you-ne"}}]},{"title":"YAML manifests and pods","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What a pod is","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>pod</strong> is the smallest thing Kubernetes runs: a wrapper around one container (sometimes a main container plus a small <strong>sidecar</strong>).</p>\n<p>Containers in the same pod:\n- always land on the <strong>same node</strong>\n- share <strong>one IP address</strong>, so they talk over <code>localhost</code>\n- can share <strong>volumes</strong> (each container must mount them)</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph POD[\"Pod · IP 10.244.1.7\"]\n    A[\"app container :8080\"]\n    S[\"sidecar (log shipper)\"]\n    V[(\"shared volume\")]\n    A --- V\n    S --- V\n  end"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Anatomy of a manifest","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every manifest has the same four top-level fields:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: v1            # which API version this kind lives in\nkind: Pod                 # what type of object\nmetadata:                 # facts ABOUT the object\n  name: nginx-pod\n  labels:\n    app: nginx            # name tags you choose\nspec:                     # the desired state\n  containers:             # a list: each item starts with \"-\"\n    - name: nginx\n      image: nginx:1.27   # pin a tag; no tag means :latest\n      ports:\n        - containerPort: 80","file":"nginx-pod.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 Not sure what goes under a field? Ask the cluster: <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Try it","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl apply -f nginx-pod.yaml\npod/nginx-pod created\n$ kubectl get pods -o wide\nNAME        READY   STATUS    IP            NODE\nnginx-pod   1/1     Running   10.244.1.7    k8s-demo-worker\n$ kubectl logs nginx-pod\n$ kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- sh\n$ kubectl delete pod nginx-pod"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>READY 1/1</code> means one of one containers is ready.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"YAML gotchas","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Spaces only, never tabs.</strong> Indentation shows what belongs to what. \"error converting YAML to JSON\" is usually one wrong indent.</li>\n<li>A pod normally has <strong>one main container</strong>. To run more copies, add more <strong>pods</strong>, not more containers.</li>\n<li>A <strong>bare pod is not self-healing</strong>: delete it and it's gone. Real work uses Deployments.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which four fields appear at the top of every Kubernetes manifest?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"name, image, ports, replicas","ok":false},{"t":"apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec","ok":true},{"t":"version, type, labels, containers","ok":false},{"t":"api, object, data, status","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>status</code> is filled in by Kubernetes. You write the other four.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m247--type-html-html-which-four-fie"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Two containers in the same pod need to talk. How?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Through a Service","ok":false},{"t":"Over <code>localhost</code>: they share one network namespace (one IP)","ok":true},{"t":"Through the Ingress","ok":false},{"t":"They can't","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Same pod = same IP and port space. They just use different ports.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m248--type-html-html-two-containers"}}]},{"title":"Labels, selectors and namespaces","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Labels and selectors","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Labels</strong> are <code>key: value</code> name tags on objects. A <strong>selector</strong> is a search: \"everything labelled <code>app: game</code>\".</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  D[\"Deployment<br/>selector app=game\"] -.owns.-> P1[\"pod<br/>app=game\"]\n  S[\"Service<br/>selector app=game\"] -.sends traffic to.-> P1\n  S -.-> P2[\"pod<br/>app=game\"]\n  S -.-x P3[\"pod<br/>app=gmae ← typo\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Deployments, Services and NetworkPolicies all find pods <strong>by label</strong>. Nothing is linked by name, so a <strong>typo means nothing connects</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl get pods --show-labels\n$ kubectl get pods -l app=game"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Namespaces","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Namespaces</strong> are folders inside one cluster, keeping groups of objects apart.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Namespace</th>\n<th>Holds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>default</code></td>\n<td>your things, unless you say otherwise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kube-system</code></td>\n<td>Kubernetes' own pods (CoreDNS, kube-proxy…)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>your own (<code>game</code>, <code>monitoring</code>, <code>cert-manager</code>…)</td>\n<td>one app or add-on each</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Use <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code> for one, <code>-A</code> for all.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Imperative vs declarative","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Imperative</th>\n<th>Declarative</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How</td>\n<td>a command says do this <strong>now</strong></td>\n<td>a file says what you <strong>want</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td><code>kubectl run</code>, <code>kubectl create deployment</code></td>\n<td><code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use for</td>\n<td>quick tests, exams, debugging</td>\n<td><strong>real work</strong>: files in Git, reviewed and repeatable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The best of both: generate a clean starting file with a command, then keep the file.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl create deploy game --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> prints the YAML and <strong>creates nothing</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <code>kubectl create pod</code> doesn't exist. Use <code>kubectl run nginx --image=nginx</code> for a quick pod.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A Service sends traffic nowhere. Its selector is <code>app: game</code>. What's the most common cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Service type is ClusterIP","ok":false},{"t":"The pods' labels don't match the selector (e.g. a typo)","ok":true},{"t":"The namespace is called default","ok":false},{"t":"etcd is down","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Selectors match labels exactly. A typo means no Endpoints, so there's nowhere to send traffic.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m249--type-html-html-a-service-send"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why is declarative (<code>kubectl apply -f</code>) the right choice for real work?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's faster to type","ok":false},{"t":"The files live in Git, so changes are reviewed, repeatable and auditable","ok":true},{"t":"Imperative commands are deprecated","ok":false},{"t":"Declarative skips validation","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The same reason infrastructure moved to Terraform: Git becomes the record of the truth.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m250--type-html-html-why-is-declara"}}]}]},{"id":"k8s-workloads","title":"Kubernetes 2: Deployments, Services & debugging","level":"Advanced","icon":"🚢","summary":"Self-healing Deployments, rolling updates and rollbacks, Services and their three ports, requests, limits, probes and a debugging routine.","topic":"Kubernetes","lessons":[{"title":"Deployments and ReplicaSets","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Why not just pods?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A bare pod that crashes <strong>stays dead</strong>. You also want several copies, and a way to change version without taking the site down. That's the <strong>Deployment</strong>'s job.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  D[\"Deployment game<br/>replicas: 3 · image game:v2\"] --> RS[\"ReplicaSet game-7d9f…<br/>keeps exactly 3\"]\n  RS --> P1[\"pod game-7d9f…-abc12\"]\n  RS --> P2[\"pod game-7d9f…-def34\"]\n  RS --> P3[\"pod game-7d9f…-ghi56\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>You talk to the <strong>Deployment</strong>. It manages a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>, and the ReplicaSet keeps the right number of <strong>pods</strong>. Pod names are deployment + ReplicaSet hash + a random suffix.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A Deployment manifest","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1           # ⚠️ apps/v1, not v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: game\n  namespace: game\nspec:\n  replicas: 3                 # how many pods to keep running\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: game               # \"the pods I own have this label\"…\n  template:                   # the blueprint for each pod\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: game             # …so this MUST match the selector\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: game\n          image: 123456789012.dkr.ecr.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/game-2048:3f9c2e1\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 8080","file":"k8s/deployment.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ \"selector does not match template labels\" means the two <code>app:</code> labels differ. The selector is also <strong>immutable</strong>: to change it, delete and recreate the Deployment.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Self-healing, scaling, updating","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl delete pod game-7d9f-abc12     # a new one appears within seconds\n$ kubectl scale deploy game --replicas=5 -n game\n$ kubectl set image deploy/game game=…/game-2048:4a1b2c3 -n game\n$ kubectl rollout status deploy/game -n game\n$ kubectl rollout history deploy/game -n game\n$ kubectl rollout undo deploy/game -n game"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <code>set image</code> is fine for practice. In real work, change the YAML (or the Helm values) and apply it, so Git stays the truth.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"What a rolling update really does","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant D as Deployment\n  participant Old as ReplicaSet v1 (3 pods)\n  participant New as ReplicaSet v2\n  D->>New: create, scale to 1\n  Note over New: waits for the readiness probe\n  D->>Old: scale to 2\n  D->>New: scale to 2\n  D->>Old: scale to 1\n  D->>New: scale to 3\n  D->>Old: scale to 0 (kept for rollback)"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The old ReplicaSet stays at <strong>0 pods</strong>, which is why <code>rollout undo</code> is instant: it just scales the old one back up. <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> control how many pods change at a time.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You delete one of a Deployment's 3 pods. What happens?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's gone; you now have 2","ok":false},{"t":"A replacement starts within seconds, because the ReplicaSet insists on 3","ok":true},{"t":"The whole Deployment restarts","ok":false},{"t":"The node is drained","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>That's self-healing. To really remove the pods, delete the <strong>Deployment</strong>.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m251--type-html-html-you-delete-one"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why is <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> so fast?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It rebuilds the old image","ok":false},{"t":"The old ReplicaSet is kept at 0 replicas, so undo just scales it back up","ok":true},{"t":"It restores etcd from a backup","ok":false},{"t":"It skips health checks","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Rolling back is a scale operation, not a rebuild.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m252--type-html-html-why-is-kubectl"}}]},{"title":"Services: stable addresses","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The problem with pod IPs","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Pods come and go, and each new pod gets a <strong>new IP</strong>. A <strong>Service</strong> gives a group of pods (selected by label) <strong>one stable name and IP</strong>, and spreads traffic across them.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  C[client pod] -->|\"http://game\"| SVC[\"Service game<br/>ClusterIP 10.96.40.12:80\"]\n  SVC -->|\":8080\"| P1[\"pod 10.244.1.7\"]\n  SVC -->|\":8080\"| P2[\"pod 10.244.2.3\"]\n  SVC -->|\":8080\"| P3[\"pod 10.244.2.9\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The current list of pod IPs behind a Service is its <strong>Endpoints</strong>. kube-proxy writes the rules on every node that make this work.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Service types","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Reachable from</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> (default)</td>\n<td>inside the cluster only</td>\n<td>pod-to-pod, and anything behind an Ingress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NodePort</strong></td>\n<td><code>&lt;any-node-IP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td>\n<td>local testing, small setups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td>\n<td>the internet, via a real cloud load balancer (an NLB/ELB on AWS)</td>\n<td>exposing one service directly, or an ingress controller</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Services work at <strong>layer 4</strong> (IPs and ports). Routing by hostname or path (layer 7) is the <strong>Ingress</strong>'s job.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The three ports","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  U[\"outside: node-IP:30080\"] -->|nodePort| SVC[\"Service<br/>port 80\"]\n  IN[\"inside: game:80\"] -->|port| SVC\n  SVC -->|targetPort 8080| POD[\"container listening on 8080\"]"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: game\n  namespace: game\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  selector:\n    app: game           # = the pods' label\n  ports:\n    - port: 80          # the Service's port\n      targetPort: 8080  # the container's port","file":"k8s/service.yaml"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Testing a Service","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"# 1) tunnel it to your laptop (testing only)\n$ kubectl port-forward svc/game 8080:80 -n game      # → http://localhost:8080\n\n# 2) from inside the cluster, with a throwaway pod full of network tools\n$ kubectl run tmp-shell --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- bash\nbash-5.2# curl http://game.game"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 Service returns nothing? <code>kubectl describe svc game -n game</code> and check <strong>Endpoints</strong>. Empty = the selector matches no pods.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A Service has <code>port: 80</code> and <code>targetPort: 8080</code>. What listens on 8080?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Service","ok":false},{"t":"The container inside the pod","ok":true},{"t":"Every node","ok":false},{"t":"The ingress controller","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>port</code> is what clients call. <code>targetPort</code> is where the container actually listens.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m253--type-html-html-a-service-has-"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which Service type is reachable <strong>only inside</strong> the cluster?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"ClusterIP","ok":true},{"t":"NodePort","ok":false},{"t":"LoadBalancer","ok":false},{"t":"ExternalName","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It's the default and the safest: put an Ingress in front for internet traffic.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m254--type-html-html-which-service-"}}]},{"title":"Resources and health probes","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Requests and limits","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>A <strong>request</strong> is what the scheduler <strong>reserves</strong> for the pod. It places the pod only where that much is free.</li>\n<li>A <strong>limit</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> it may use.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: 50m          # 50 millicores = 0.05 of a CPU core\n    memory: 64Mi\n  limits:\n    memory: 128Mi     # over this → the container is killed (OOMKilled)"},{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Over the limit</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>memory</strong></td>\n<td>container <strong>killed</strong> (<code>OOMKilled</code>, exit 137)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CPU</strong></td>\n<td>only <strong>slowed down</strong> (throttled)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Many teams set a memory limit but no CPU limit. A pod stuck <code>Pending</code> with \"Insufficient cpu\" means the requests don't fit on any node.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Readiness vs liveness","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  R{\"readiness probe<br/>ready for traffic?\"} -->|no| OUT[\"removed from the Service<br/>(no traffic, not restarted)\"]\n  L{\"liveness probe<br/>still alive?\"} -->|no| RST[\"container restarted\"]"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"readinessProbe:\n  httpGet: { path: /, port: 8080 }\n  initialDelaySeconds: 3\n  periodSeconds: 5\nlivenessProbe:\n  httpGet: { path: /, port: 8080 }\n  initialDelaySeconds: 10\n  periodSeconds: 10"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Rolling updates <strong>wait for readiness</strong>, so a broken version never receives traffic.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Pointing liveness at something slow or dependent (like a database) can cause restart loops. Keep it simple.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A container keeps showing <code>OOMKilled</code>. What happened?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It used too much CPU","ok":false},{"t":"It went over its memory limit and was killed","ok":true},{"t":"Its readiness probe failed","ok":false},{"t":"The image couldn't be pulled","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Raise the memory limit (and request), or fix the memory growth. CPU over the limit only throttles.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m255--type-html-html-a-container-ke"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod is still warming up and shouldn't get traffic yet. Which probe handles this?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Readiness","ok":true},{"t":"Liveness","ok":false},{"t":"Startup quota","ok":false},{"t":"PodDisruptionBudget","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Readiness controls <strong>traffic</strong>. Liveness controls <strong>restarts</strong>.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m256--type-html-html-a-pod-is-still"}}]},{"title":"Debugging with kubectl","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The toolkit","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl get pods\\|deploy\\|svc\\|all -n ns</code></td>\n<td>list (<code>-o wide</code>, <code>-o yaml</code>, <code>-w</code> to watch)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl describe &lt;type&gt; &lt;name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>full details + <strong>Events</strong>: your first stop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; [-f] [--previous]</code></td>\n<td>what the container printed (and the last crash)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh</code></td>\n<td>shell inside (<code>--</code> ends kubectl's own flags)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl get events -n ns --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code></td>\n<td>what's been happening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kubectl explain &lt;field&gt;</code></td>\n<td>built-in docs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Short names: <code>po</code>, <code>deploy</code>, <code>rs</code>, <code>svc</code>, <code>cm</code>, <code>ns</code>, <code>ing</code>. Many people <code>alias k=kubectl</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Pod statuses decoded","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Usually means</th>\n<th>Look at</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Pending</code></td>\n<td>no node has room (requests) or it's waiting for storage</td>\n<td><code>describe pod</code> → Events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ImagePullBackOff</code></td>\n<td>wrong image name or tag, or no permission to pull</td>\n<td><code>describe pod</code> → Events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code></td>\n<td>starts, crashes, repeat (with growing waits)</td>\n<td><code>logs --previous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OOMKilled</code></td>\n<td>over the memory limit</td>\n<td><code>describe pod</code> → Last State</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Running</code> but not <code>READY</code></td>\n<td>readiness probe failing</td>\n<td><code>describe pod</code> → probe errors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"A debugging routine","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  A[\"kubectl get pods<br/>what's the status?\"] --> B[\"kubectl describe pod<br/>read the Events\"]\n  B --> C[\"kubectl logs --previous<br/>what did the app say?\"]\n  C --> D[\"kubectl describe svc<br/>any Endpoints?\"]\n  D --> E[\"netshoot pod: curl the Service<br/>from inside\"]\n  E --> F[\"kubectl exec into the pod<br/>look around\"]"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod is <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. Which command shows why the <strong>last</strong> run crashed?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>kubectl get pods -w</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>kubectl describe node</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>kubectl rollout status</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The current container may have only just started. <code>--previous</code> shows the one that died.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m257--type-html-html-a-pod-is-crash"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. What's the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Not enough memory","ok":false},{"t":"A wrong image name or tag, or no permission to pull from the registry","ok":true},{"t":"A failing liveness probe","ok":false},{"t":"A NetworkPolicy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>On EKS, the node role needs ECR read access (the EKS module adds it) and the tag must exist.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m258--type-html-html-a-pod-is-stuck"}}]}]},{"id":"k8s-config-network","title":"Kubernetes 3: storage, config & networking","level":"Advanced","icon":"🧩","summary":"PersistentVolumes and claims, ConfigMaps, Secrets, the pod network and CoreDNS, NetworkPolicies, and Ingress with its controllers.","topic":"Kubernetes","lessons":[{"title":"Persistent storage","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Pods are ephemeral","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Anything a container writes to its own file system <strong>vanishes with the pod</strong>. Databases, uploads and queues need storage that outlives pods.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Same lesson as the ECS example:</strong> Memos keeps SQLite inside the container, so every redeploy loses it. On Kubernetes the fix is a PVC; on ECS it's EFS or RDS.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"PV, PVC and StorageClass","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  POD[\"Pod<br/>volumeMounts /data\"] --> PVC[\"PersistentVolumeClaim<br/>'I need 1Gi, RWO'\"]\n  PVC -->|bound| PV[\"PersistentVolume<br/>a real disk (e.g. EBS)\"]\n  SC[\"StorageClass<br/>(e.g. gp3)\"] -.creates on demand.-> PV"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>PV:</strong> a piece of storage in the cluster (EBS, EFS, NFS…).</li>\n<li><strong>PVC:</strong> an app's <strong>request</strong> for storage. Kubernetes binds it to a matching PV.</li>\n<li><strong>StorageClass:</strong> a recipe that creates PVs <strong>automatically</strong> when a PVC asks (dynamic provisioning).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think of a library: the PV is a locker, the PVC is your request form, and the StorageClass is a locker factory.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Access modes","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Typical AWS storage</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ReadWriteOnce (RWO)</td>\n<td>read-write by <strong>one node</strong></td>\n<td>EBS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReadOnlyMany (ROX)</td>\n<td>read-only by many nodes</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReadWriteMany (RWX)</td>\n<td>read-write by many nodes</td>\n<td>EFS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ RWO means one <strong>node</strong>, not one pod.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Using a claim","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata: { name: data, namespace: app }\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]\n  storageClassName: gp3\n  resources: { requests: { storage: 1Gi } }\n---\n# in the pod spec\ncontainers:\n  - name: app\n    volumeMounts:\n      - name: data\n        mountPath: /data\nvolumes:\n  - name: data\n    persistentVolumeClaim:\n      claimName: data"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>On EKS, PVCs need the <strong>EBS CSI driver</strong> add-on (with its IAM role) and a StorageClass. Without them, PVCs stay <code>Pending</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A PVC is stuck in <code>Pending</code>. Which is the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The pod's image is wrong","ok":false},{"t":"No PV matches (size, access mode, class) and nothing can create one, e.g. the EBS CSI driver isn't installed on EKS","ok":true},{"t":"The Service has no Endpoints","ok":false},{"t":"The namespace is wrong for CoreDNS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Dynamic provisioning needs a StorageClass <strong>and</strong> a working CSI driver.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m259--type-html-html-a-pvc-is-stuck"}}]},{"title":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"ConfigMaps: settings outside the image","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>ConfigMap</strong> holds non-secret config, so <strong>one image works in every environment</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  IMG[\"same image\"] --> DEV[\"dev pod<br/>+ ConfigMap MODE=dev\"]\n  IMG --> PROD[\"prod pod<br/>+ ConfigMap MODE=prod\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod can use it in three ways:\n1. one key as an env var (<code>configMapKeyRef</code>)\n2. all keys as env vars (<code>envFrom</code>)\n3. as files in a folder (a volume), where each key becomes a file</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 Env vars are read <strong>once, at start-up</strong>: restart the pod to see changes. <strong>Mounted files update</strong> when the ConfigMap changes.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Secrets: base64 is not encryption","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>Secret</strong> is used like a ConfigMap, but kept separate so access can be restricted.</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl create secret generic db --from-literal=password=s3cret\n$ kubectl get secret db -o jsonpath='{.data.password}'\nczNjcmV0\n$ echo czNjcmV0 | base64 -d\ns3cret          ← anyone who can read the Secret can decode it"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Protect Secrets with:\n- <strong>RBAC</strong> (who may read them)\n- <strong>etcd encryption at rest</strong> (EKS can use KMS for this)\n- <strong>never committing them to Git</strong>\n- often an external store: AWS Secrets Manager or Vault (e.g. via the External Secrets Operator)</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A teammate says \"our Secrets are safe, they're encrypted\". What's the correction?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're right: Secrets are AES-encrypted by default","ok":false},{"t":"Secret values are only base64-encoded; protect them with RBAC, encryption at rest and an external store, and never commit them","ok":true},{"t":"Secrets can't be read by anyone","ok":false},{"t":"Only ConfigMaps are encoded","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>base64 -d</code> decodes them in one command.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m260--type-html-html-a-teammate-say"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You changed a ConfigMap, but the app still shows the old value it reads from an <strong>environment variable</strong>. Why?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"ConfigMaps are cached for 24 hours","ok":false},{"t":"Env vars are only read when the container starts, so restart the pod","ok":true},{"t":"The ConfigMap is in the wrong namespace","ok":false},{"t":"ConfigMaps can't be changed","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Mounted files would update, but environment variables don't.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m261--type-html-html-you-changed-a-"}}]},{"title":"The pod network and DNS","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The network model","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li>Every pod gets <strong>its own IP</strong>.</li>\n<li>Any pod can reach any other pod <strong>directly</strong>, with no NAT, even on another node.</li>\n<li>Everything is <strong>open</strong> until you add NetworkPolicies.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A <strong>CNI plugin</strong> builds this network: Calico, Cilium, Flannel, or the <strong>AWS VPC CNI</strong> on EKS (which gives pods real VPC IP addresses).</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph N1[\"node A\"]\n    A[\"pod 10.0.1.23\"]\n  end\n  subgraph N2[\"node B\"]\n    B[\"pod 10.0.2.41\"]\n  end\n  A <-->|direct, no NAT| B"}]},{"type":"card","title":"CoreDNS: the cluster's phone book","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every Service gets DNS names from <strong>CoreDNS</strong> (it shows up as <code>kube-dns</code> in <code>kube-system</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Works from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>game</code></td>\n<td>pods in the <strong>same</strong> namespace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>game.game</code> (<code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;</code>)</td>\n<td>any namespace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>game.game.svc.cluster.local</code></td>\n<td>anywhere (the FQDN)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Short names work because of the <strong>search list</strong> in each pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant P as pod (ns: web)\n  participant D as CoreDNS\n  participant S as Service api.backend\n  P->>D: who is api.backend.svc.cluster.local?\n  D-->>P: ClusterIP 10.96.12.5\n  P->>S: GET / → kube-proxy picks a backend pod"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod in namespace <code>web</code> wants the Service <code>api</code> in namespace <code>backend</code>. Which name works?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>api</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>api.backend</code> (or the full <code>api.backend.svc.cluster.local</code>)","ok":true},{"t":"<code>backend.api</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>api.web</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The short name <code>api</code> would only resolve inside the <code>backend</code> namespace.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m262--type-html-html-a-pod-in-names"}}]},{"title":"NetworkPolicies","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Firewalls for pods","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> picks pods by label and controls traffic <strong>in</strong> (ingress) and/or <strong>out</strong> (egress).</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata: { name: db-allow-api, namespace: shop }\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels: { app: db }          # WHICH pods this protects\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels: { app: api } # WHO may call them\n      ports:\n        - { protocol: TCP, port: 5432 }"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  API[\"pod app=api\"] -->|5432 ✅| DB[\"pod app=db\"]\n  WEB[\"pod app=web\"] -.-x|blocked| DB"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Two rules to remember","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🧠 Once <strong>any</strong> policy selects a pod, everything <strong>not explicitly allowed</strong> is denied (for that direction).</p>\n<p>⚠️ Policies only work if the CNI <strong>enforces</strong> them (Calico, Cilium, or the AWS VPC CNI with network policy enabled). Otherwise they're <strong>silently ignored</strong>: no error, and no protection.</p>\n<p>🔧 It's the Kubernetes version of \"the ECS task accepts 8081 only from the ALB's security group\".</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You apply a NetworkPolicy and nothing gets blocked. What's the likely reason?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Policies take 24 hours to apply","ok":false},{"t":"The CNI plugin doesn't enforce NetworkPolicies, so they're silently ignored","ok":true},{"t":"You need a LoadBalancer Service","ok":false},{"t":"NetworkPolicies only work on ports below 1024","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Check that your CNI supports and has policy enforcement turned on.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m263--type-html-html-you-apply-a-ne"}}]},{"title":"Ingress and ingress controllers","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"One door, many apps","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service per app means one paid cloud load balancer per app, and none of them can route by hostname or path. An <strong>Ingress</strong> is a set of <strong>layer-7 rules</strong> (host/path → Service) served through <strong>one</strong> entry point.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  U((users)) --> LB[\"one cloud load balancer\"] --> IC[\"ingress controller pods<br/>(NGINX)\"]\n  IC -->|\"game.lab.example.com\"| S1[Service game]\n  IC -->|\"grafana.lab.example.com\"| S2[Service grafana]\n  IC -->|\"argocd.lab.example.com\"| S3[Service argocd]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Rules vs the controller","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Ingress resource</strong> is just the list of delivery rules.</li>\n<li>The <strong>ingress controller</strong> is the program (pods) that reads the rules and routes the traffic.</li>\n<li><strong>No controller = the Ingress does nothing.</strong></li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: game\n  namespace: game\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: nginx        # which controller follows these rules\n  rules:\n    - host: game.lab.example.com\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend:\n              service:\n                name: game\n                port:\n                  number: 80","file":"k8s/ingress.yaml"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Which NGINX controller?","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Two different products share almost the same name:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Community <code>ingress-nginx</code></th>\n<th>F5 NGINX Ingress Controller</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Made by</td>\n<td>Kubernetes community</td>\n<td>F5 / NGINX</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Status</td>\n<td><strong>retired</strong> (best-effort maintenance ended in March 2026)</td>\n<td>maintained, open source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Annotations</td>\n<td><code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/…</code></td>\n<td><code>nginx.org/…</code> (different!)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Kubernetes project also recommends the <strong>Gateway API</strong>, Ingress's more expressive successor, for new work.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Annotations copied from old ingress-nginx tutorials do <strong>nothing</strong> on F5's controller.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You create an Ingress and nothing happens. Why?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Ingresses take an hour to propagate","ok":false},{"t":"No ingress controller is installed, or <code>ingressClassName</code> names one that doesn't exist","ok":true},{"t":"The Service must be type LoadBalancer","ok":false},{"t":"Ingress only supports HTTP/1.0","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>An Ingress is only rules. Something has to follow them.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m264--type-html-html-you-create-an-"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why put many apps behind one Ingress instead of one LoadBalancer Service each?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Ingress is more secure by default","ok":false},{"t":"One load balancer serves them all (cheaper), and it can route by hostname and path (layer 7)","ok":true},{"t":"LoadBalancer Services don't work on AWS","ok":false},{"t":"Ingress removes the need for Services","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Ingress still sends traffic to <strong>Services</strong>. It just shares one front door.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m265--type-html-html-why-put-many-a"}}]}]},{"id":"k8s-eks","title":"Kubernetes 4: EKS, Helm, GitOps & monitoring","level":"Advanced","icon":"🛳️","summary":"A production-style EKS cluster with Terraform, access entries and IRSA, Helm, cert-manager, ExternalDNS, Argo CD, Prometheus and Grafana, secure pipelines and clean-up.","topic":"Kubernetes","lessons":[{"title":"EKS with Terraform","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"What EKS gives you","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Amazon EKS</strong> is managed Kubernetes. AWS runs the <strong>control plane</strong> (API server, etcd, scheduler) across three AZs. You run <strong>worker nodes</strong>, usually in a <strong>managed node group</strong> (EC2 instances EKS creates and updates for you).</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  subgraph AWS[\"AWS-managed\"]\n    CP[\"EKS control plane<br/>API server · etcd · scheduler\"]\n  end\n  subgraph VPC[\"Your VPC\"]\n    subgraph PUB[\"public subnets\"]\n      NLB[\"load balancers\"]\n      NAT[NAT gateway]\n    end\n    subgraph PRIV[\"private subnets\"]\n      N1[\"node (EC2)\"] & N2[\"node (EC2)\"]\n    end\n  end\n  CP <--> N1 & N2\n  NLB --> N1 & N2\n  N1 & N2 -.outbound.-> NAT"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>EKS isn't free.</strong> The control plane alone is about $0.10/hour (~$73/month). Nodes, a NAT gateway and a load balancer come on top. Set a <strong>budget alert</strong> and <code>terraform destroy</code> after each practice session.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"VPC and EKS as modules","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Community modules build dozens of resources per block. Wrapping them in <strong>your own</strong> module (your inputs, sensible defaults) still counts as a reusable module.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"module \"vpc\" {\n  source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\"\n  version = \"~> 6.0\"\n\n  name            = var.name\n  cidr            = var.cidr                      # e.g. 10.0.0.0/16\n  azs             = var.azs                       # 3 AZs\n  private_subnets = var.private_subnets           # worker nodes\n  public_subnets  = var.public_subnets            # load balancers\n\n  enable_nat_gateway = true\n  single_nat_gateway = var.single_nat_gateway     # true = cheap, lab only\n\n  public_subnet_tags  = { \"kubernetes.io/role/elb\" = 1 }           # public LBs land here\n  private_subnet_tags = { \"kubernetes.io/role/internal-elb\" = 1 }\n}","file":"terraform/modules/vpc/main.tf"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The cluster","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"module \"eks\" {\n  source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws\"\n  version = \"~> 21.0\"\n\n  name               = var.name\n  kubernetes_version = var.kubernetes_version   # pick a version in standard support\n  vpc_id             = var.vpc_id\n  subnet_ids         = var.private_subnet_ids   # nodes in private subnets\n\n  endpoint_public_access                   = true   # lab only: lock down in production\n  enable_cluster_creator_admin_permissions = true   # whoever applies gets admin\n  enable_irsa                              = true   # pods can get their own IAM roles\n\n  eks_managed_node_groups = {\n    default = {\n      instance_types = [\"t3.large\"]\n      min_size       = 1\n      max_size       = 3\n      desired_size   = 2\n    }\n  }\n}","file":"terraform/modules/eks/main.tf"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Module major versions rename inputs (v21 uses <code>name</code> and <code>kubernetes_version</code>, not <code>cluster_name</code>/<code>cluster_version</code>). Always read the module's upgrade notes.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Getting kubectl in: access entries","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ aws eks update-kubeconfig --region eu-west-2 --name eks-lab\n$ kubectl get nodes\nerror: You must be logged in to the server (Unauthorized)"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Being able to call <strong>AWS</strong> doesn't mean <strong>Kubernetes</strong> lets you in. Every IAM identity needs an <strong>access entry</strong> with an access policy:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"access_entries = {\n  github_actions = {\n    principal_arn = var.github_actions_role_arn\n    policy_associations = {\n      admin = {\n        policy_arn   = \"arn:aws:eks::aws:cluster-access-policy/AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy\"\n        access_scope = { type = \"cluster\" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 Admin is fine for a lab. In production, use something narrower, like <code>AmazonEKSEditPolicy</code> scoped to one namespace.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"IRSA: AWS permissions for one app's pods","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Without it, every pod on a node shares the <strong>node's</strong> IAM role. <strong>IRSA</strong> (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) ties an IAM role to <strong>one Kubernetes service account</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant P as pod (sa: external-dns)\n  participant OIDC as EKS OIDC provider\n  participant STS as AWS STS\n  participant R53 as Route 53\n  P->>OIDC: projected token for sa external-dns/external-dns\n  P->>STS: AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity(role from SA annotation)\n  STS-->>P: temporary creds (only this role's permissions)\n  P->>R53: ChangeResourceRecordSets (one zone only)"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The service account carries the annotation <code>eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: &lt;role ARN&gt;</code>. The role's trust policy only accepts <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;sa-name&gt;</code>, so <strong>the wrong namespace means no AWS access</strong>. <strong>EKS Pod Identity</strong> is a newer, simpler alternative.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 It's the same idea as the ECS <strong>task role</strong>, and the same OIDC trust pattern as GitHub Actions.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code> works, but <code>kubectl get nodes</code> says \"You must be logged in to the server\". What's missing?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The kubeconfig file","ok":false},{"t":"An EKS access entry (with an access policy) for your IAM identity","ok":true},{"t":"A NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"The node group's IAM role","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>AWS authentication ≠ Kubernetes authorisation. Add an access entry, or set <code>enable_cluster_creator_admin_permissions</code>.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m266--type-html-html-aws-sts-get-ca"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does IRSA give you over putting permissions on the node role?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Faster pods","ok":false},{"t":"Each app's pods get only their own role's permissions, instead of every pod on the node sharing them","ok":true},{"t":"Free IAM","ok":false},{"t":"It replaces RBAC","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Least privilege per workload, scoped to a namespace and service account.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m267--type-html-html-what-does-irsa"}}]},{"title":"Helm: the package manager","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Charts, values and releases","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Real add-ons are dozens of Kubernetes objects. <strong>Helm</strong> installs them as one <strong>chart</strong>, customised by a <strong>values file</strong>. Each installed copy is a <strong>release</strong>.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  CH[\"chart<br/>templates with {{ }} blanks\"] --> H{helm}\n  V[\"values.yaml<br/>your settings\"] --> H\n  H -->|renders| Y[\"plain Kubernetes YAML\"] --> K[cluster]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The commands you'll use","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm repo add &lt;name&gt; &lt;url&gt;</code> / <code>helm repo update</code></td>\n<td>add / refresh a chart repository</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm show values &lt;repo&gt;/&lt;chart&gt;</code></td>\n<td>see every setting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm upgrade --install &lt;rel&gt; &lt;chart&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --create-namespace -f values.yaml</code></td>\n<td>install <strong>or</strong> upgrade (what pipelines use)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm list -A</code> / <code>helm history &lt;rel&gt;</code></td>\n<td>what's installed / its revisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm rollback &lt;rel&gt; &lt;rev&gt;</code></td>\n<td>go back a revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>helm template</code> / <code>helm lint</code></td>\n<td>render YAML / check a chart <strong>without</strong> installing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Your own chart","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ helm create game-2048\ngame-2048/\n├── Chart.yaml        # name and version\n├── values.yaml       # default values for the blanks\n└── templates/\n    ├── deployment.yaml\n    ├── service.yaml\n    └── ingress.yaml"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"spec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: game\n          image: \"{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}\"","file":"game-2048/templates/deployment.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The pipeline then runs <code>helm upgrade --install game ./game-2048 --set image.tag=$GITHUB_SHA</code>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Installing charts from Terraform","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"hcl","code":"resource \"helm_release\" \"cert_manager\" {\n  name             = \"cert-manager\"\n  repository       = \"https://charts.jetstack.io\"\n  chart            = \"cert-manager\"\n  namespace        = \"cert-manager\"\n  create_namespace = true\n  # version = \"…\"   ← pin the chart version you tested\n  set = [{ name = \"crds.enabled\", value = \"true\" }]   # Helm provider v3 list syntax\n}"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ The Helm and Kubernetes providers need a cluster that <strong>already exists</strong>. Many teams put add-ons in a <strong>second Terraform stack</strong> applied after the cluster.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which Helm command installs a chart if it's missing and upgrades it if it's there?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>helm install --force</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>helm upgrade --install</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>helm apply</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>helm sync</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>That's why pipelines use it: the same command works on the first run and every run after.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m268--type-html-html-which-helm-com"}}]},{"title":"Automatic HTTPS and DNS","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The full request path","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  U([browser]) -->|\"game.lab.example.com\"| R53[\"Route 53<br/>(record made by ExternalDNS)\"]\n  R53 --> NLB[\"NLB (layer 4)<br/>passes TLS through\"]\n  NLB --> IC[\"NGINX ingress controller<br/>ends TLS with the cert-manager Secret\"]\n  IC --> SVC[Service game] --> P[game pods]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Delegating a subdomain","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You can keep the domain at its registrar and hand <strong>one subdomain</strong> to Route 53:\n1. Route 53 → create a public hosted zone <code>lab.example.com</code> (AWS gives it 4 NS records).\n2. At the registrar, add those 4 <strong>NS</strong> records for <code>lab</code>.\n3. Check: <code>dig NS lab.example.com +short</code> shows the AWS name servers.</p>\n<p>ExternalDNS and cert-manager are then only allowed to edit <strong>that zone</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"cert-manager: free, self-renewing certificates","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>cert-manager asks <strong>Let's Encrypt</strong> for certificates and proves you own the domain with a <strong>DNS-01 challenge</strong>: a TXT record in Route 53.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant I as Ingress (tls + annotation)\n  participant CM as cert-manager (IRSA)\n  participant LE as Let's Encrypt\n  participant R as Route 53\n  I->>CM: needs a cert for game.lab.example.com\n  CM->>LE: order certificate\n  LE-->>CM: prove it: TXT _acme-challenge…\n  CM->>R: create TXT record\n  LE->>R: check TXT\n  LE-->>CM: issued (90 days)\n  CM->>I: saved in Secret game-tls (renews ~30 days before expiry)"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1\nkind: ClusterIssuer\nmetadata: { name: letsencrypt }\nspec:\n  acme:\n    server: https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory   # staging first!\n    email: you@example.com\n    privateKeySecretRef: { name: letsencrypt }\n    solvers:\n      - dns01:\n          route53: { region: eu-west-2 }\n        selector:\n          dnsZones: [lab.example.com]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Test with Let's Encrypt <strong>staging</strong> (production has strict rate limits), then switch the server URL.</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"ExternalDNS: records from Ingresses","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>ExternalDNS</strong> watches Ingresses (and Services) and creates the matching Route 53 records, plus TXT \"ownership\" records so it only ever changes records <strong>it</strong> made.</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"provider:\n  name: aws\ndomainFilters: [lab.example.com]   # only ever touch this zone\npolicy: sync                       # also delete records for removed Ingresses\ntxtOwnerId: eks-lab\nserviceAccount:\n  annotations:\n    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/external-dns","file":"helm-values/external-dns.yaml"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>An Ingress then only needs <code>cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt</code> and a <code>tls:</code> block. The certificate <strong>and</strong> the DNS record appear automatically.</p>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How does cert-manager prove you own the domain in this setup?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"By emailing the domain owner","ok":false},{"t":"A DNS-01 challenge: it creates a <code>_acme-challenge</code> TXT record in Route 53, which Let's Encrypt checks","ok":true},{"t":"By uploading a file to S3","ok":false},{"t":"Route 53 trusts EKS automatically","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It writes to Route 53 using its own IRSA role, limited to one hosted zone.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m269--type-html-html-how-does-cert-"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The ingress controller sits behind an NLB. Where does TLS end?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"At the NLB","ok":false},{"t":"At the ingress controller, using the certificate cert-manager stored in a Secret","ok":true},{"t":"Inside each game pod","ok":false},{"t":"At Route 53","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The NLB works at layer 4 and passes encrypted traffic straight through. The controller does the layer-7 work.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m270--type-html-html-the-ingress-co"}}]},{"title":"GitOps with Argo CD","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Push vs pull deployment","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  subgraph PUSH[\"Push (CI deploys)\"]\n    G1[git push] --> CI[pipeline] -->|kubectl apply| K1[cluster]\n  end\n  subgraph PULL[\"Pull (GitOps)\"]\n    G2[git push] --> REPO[(Git repo)]\n    A[Argo CD in the cluster] -->|watches| REPO\n    A -->|syncs| K2[cluster]\n  end"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>With <strong>GitOps</strong>, Git is the single source of truth, and a tool <strong>inside the cluster</strong> keeps it matching Git. The pipeline no longer needs cluster credentials, which is safer.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"An Application","blocks":[{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: game\n  namespace: argocd              # must be Argo CD's own namespace\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://github.com/your-username/eks-assignment\n    targetRevision: main\n    path: k8s                    # or a Helm chart folder\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: game\n  syncPolicy:\n    automated:\n      prune: true                # delete what was removed from Git\n      selfHeal: true             # undo manual changes\n    syncOptions: [CreateNamespace=true]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Sync</strong> = apply Git's changes. <strong>Prune</strong> = delete what was removed from Git. <strong>Self-heal</strong> = undo manual edits.</li>\n<li>First admin password: <code>kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath=\"{.data.password}\" | base64 -d</code></li>\n<li>Private repo errors (\"authentication required\")? Add the repo's credentials in Argo CD.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do <code>prune</code> and <code>selfHeal</code> do in Argo CD?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Shrink images and restart pods","ok":false},{"t":"Prune deletes resources removed from Git; selfHeal reverts changes made directly in the cluster","ok":true},{"t":"They schedule backups","ok":false},{"t":"They're only for Helm charts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Together they keep the cluster exactly matching the repo.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m271--type-html-html-what-do-prune-"}}]},{"title":"Monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"How it fits together","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  NE[\"node-exporter<br/>(each node: CPU, mem, disk)\"] --> PR[(Prometheus<br/>scrapes + stores)]\n  KSM[\"kube-state-metrics<br/>(pods, deployments, restarts)\"] --> PR\n  KL[\"kubelet / cAdvisor<br/>(container usage)\"] --> PR\n  PR --> GR[\"Grafana<br/>dashboards\"]\n  PR --> AM[\"Alertmanager<br/>alerts\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Prometheus</strong> pulls (<strong>scrapes</strong>) metrics from <strong>exporters</strong> and stores them over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Grafana</strong> draws dashboards from them.</li>\n<li><strong>Alertmanager</strong> sends alerts when a rule fires.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>kube-prometheus-stack</strong> Helm chart installs all of it, with ready-made dashboards.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Getting in","blocks":[{"type":"output","text":"$ kubectl get pods -n monitoring\n$ kubectl get secret -n monitoring monitoring-grafana -o jsonpath=\"{.data.admin-password}\" | base64 -d\n$ kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/monitoring-grafana 3000:80    # http://localhost:3000"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Built-in dashboards include <strong>Kubernetes / Compute Resources / Cluster</strong> (CPU and memory by namespace) and <strong>Node Exporter / Nodes</strong>.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"PromQL for your own panels","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Panel</th>\n<th>Query</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Running pods per namespace</td>\n<td><code>sum by (namespace) (kube_pod_status_phase{phase=\"Running\"})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of nodes</td>\n<td><code>count(kube_node_info)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPU by namespace</td>\n<td><code>sum by (namespace) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{container!=\"\"}[5m]))</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memory by namespace</td>\n<td><code>sum by (namespace) (container_memory_working_set_bytes{container!=\"\"})</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Without the EBS CSI driver and a StorageClass, Prometheus keeps data inside the pod (lost on restart). The stack is also heavy: on small nodes pods sit <code>Pending</code> with \"Insufficient memory\".</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which component <strong>draws the dashboards</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Prometheus","ok":false},{"t":"Grafana","ok":true},{"t":"Alertmanager","ok":false},{"t":"kube-state-metrics","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Prometheus collects and stores, Grafana visualises, Alertmanager notifies.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m272--type-html-html-which-componen"}}]},{"title":"Secure pipelines for Kubernetes","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The secure delivery order","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[\"Checkov<br/>(code)\"] --> B[\"docker build\"] --> C[\"Trivy<br/>(image)\"] --> D[\"push to ECR<br/>:git-sha\"] --> E[\"deploy<br/>kubectl / Helm / Argo CD\"] --> F{\"rollout status<br/>healthy?\"}\n  F -->|no| G[\"rollout undo<br/>+ fail the run\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A vulnerable image <strong>never reaches the registry</strong>, and a broken release rolls itself back.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Hardening a container","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Checkov commonly flags missing <strong>requests/limits</strong>, missing <strong>probes</strong>, running as <strong>root</strong>, the <strong><code>latest</code></strong> tag and no <strong>securityContext</strong>:</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"securityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true      # mount an emptyDir at /tmp if the app needs to write\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [\"ALL\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>To accept a finding deliberately: <code># checkov:skip=CKV_K8S_xx: reason</code>. Always write the reason.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Clean-up: inside out","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Kubernetes itself creates some AWS resources (the <strong>NLB</strong>, <strong>DNS records</strong>, <strong>EBS volumes</strong>). Terraform doesn't know about them, so destroying the cluster first leaves <strong>orphans</strong> that keep billing and can block the VPC from being deleted.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[\"1. delete Argo CD apps<br/>(Ingresses, DNS records go)\"] --> B[\"2. helm uninstall add-ons<br/>(the controller's NLB goes)\"] --> C[\"3. terraform destroy\"] --> D[\"4. check EC2 → Load Balancers,<br/>Route 53, EBS volumes\"]"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You ran <code>terraform destroy</code> first and it hung deleting the VPC. Why?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Terraform can't delete VPCs","ok":false},{"t":"Kubernetes created resources Terraform doesn't track (e.g. the ingress controller's NLB), which still use the VPC","ok":true},{"t":"The state file was locked","ok":false},{"t":"The NAT gateway was in use by Grafana","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Clean up from the inside out: apps → Helm releases → Terraform → check the console.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m273--type-html-html-you-ran-terraf"}},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why scan with Trivy <strong>before</strong> pushing to ECR?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Trivy can't scan images in ECR","ok":false},{"t":"So a vulnerable image never reaches the registry or the cluster","ok":true},{"t":"It's faster","ok":false},{"t":"ECR requires it","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Fail early and cheaply. Scan-on-push in ECR is a second layer, not a replacement.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m274--type-html-html-why-scan-with-"}}]}]},{"id":"interview","title":"Interview mastery: present what you built","level":"Advanced","icon":"🎤","summary":"Turn the project you built in the Lab into a 60-second pitch, strong stories, an improvement roadmap and calm answers to hard questions.","topic":"Interview","lessons":[{"title":"The pitch","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Your project is now an interview asset","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Once you've finished the <strong>Build Lab</strong>, you have a real, working platform to talk about. Interviewers love a project you can explain end to end, because it shows you can do the job, not just define the words.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The 60-second version","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Adapt this template to what you actually built, then say it out loud until it's natural:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“I deployed the open-source <strong>Memos</strong> app to AWS at <strong>memos.example.com</strong>. It runs on <strong>ECS Fargate in private subnets</strong> across <strong>two AZs</strong>, behind a public <strong>ALB</strong> that redirects HTTP to HTTPS using an <strong>ACM</strong> certificate validated automatically through <strong>Route 53</strong>. The image is a <strong>three-stage Docker build</strong>, 2.79 GB down to 75.8 MB, and runs as a non-root user. Everything is <strong>Terraform</strong>: a bootstrap stack for state and OIDC, then seven modules, with <strong>S3 remote state and native locking</strong>. <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> builds and deploys with <strong>OIDC</strong>, so there are no stored AWS keys. tfsec scans every run, a post-deploy check hits <strong>/healthz</strong>, and I can <strong>roll back</strong> by redeploying any previous commit SHA.”</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"The diagram you should be able to draw","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart TB\n  U([User]) --> R53[Route 53 alias] --> ALB[\"ALB · public subnets<br/>80→301→443 · ACM\"]\n  ALB -->|8081 · SG→SG| T[\"ECS Fargate task · private subnets<br/>non-root · 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB\"]\n  T --> NAT[NAT per AZ] --> ECR[(ECR · immutable SHA tags)]\n  T --> CW[CloudWatch /ecs/memos]\n  GH[\"GitHub Actions · OIDC\"] -->|push image| ECR\n  GH -->|terraform apply| TF[(S3 state + lockfile)]"},{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>🔧 <strong>Tip:</strong> practise drawing it on paper in under two minutes. Whiteboard rounds often start with \"draw me your project\".</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Know your known limits","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Say these <strong>before</strong> the interviewer finds them. It shows maturity:\n1. SQLite data lives <strong>inside</strong> the container, so it's lost when the task is replaced.\n2. <strong>One task</strong>: not truly highly available.\n3. The OIDC role is <strong>admin</strong>, and the trust allows any branch.\n4. The target group checks <code>/</code>, not <code>/healthz</code>.\n5. No alarms, flow logs or WAF yet (cost trade-offs, documented).</p>"}]}]},{"title":"Telling stories","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"The story structure","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Use <strong>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</strong>. Keep each to about 90 seconds.</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  S[Situation] --> P[Problem] --> F[How I found it] --> X[Fix] --> L[\"Lesson ⭐<br/>(what you do differently now)\"]"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <strong>lesson</strong> is what interviewers remember. It shows you learn.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Eight problems you'll probably hit","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Story</th>\n<th>Lesson</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>OIDC wouldn't authenticate</td>\n<td>the trust policy <code>sub</code> must match exactly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>.gitignore</code> hid <code>cmd/memos/main.go</code></td>\n<td>anchor patterns: <code>/memos</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Go 1.23 image vs newer <code>go.mod</code></td>\n<td>match toolchain versions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Port drift 5230 ↔ 8081</td>\n<td>define the port once</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>CGO needed C libraries</td>\n<td><code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> → static binary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>2.79 GB image</td>\n<td>multi-stage → 75.8 MB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Immutable tags broke re-runs</td>\n<td>check ECR first, reuse the image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>ACM records already existed</td>\n<td><code>import</code> blocks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>People building this project commonly hit these. If <strong>you</strong> hit one while building, that's your story: tell it with the real details. Have two or three ready.</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Example answer: the OIDC story","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Situation:</strong> the first pipeline run deploying the app with GitHub Actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem:</strong> the AWS login step failed. The role couldn't be assumed.</li>\n<li><strong>Found it:</strong> I checked the token's claims against the role's trust policy and saw the <code>sub</code> didn't match my repo name.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix:</strong> I corrected the trust policy (<code>aud = sts.amazonaws.com</code>, <code>sub</code> like <code>repo:your-username/memos-aws-infra:ref:refs/heads/main</code>), moved it into the bootstrap stack, and kept everything keyless, with no static keys as a workaround.</li>\n<li><strong>Lesson:</strong> OIDC failures are almost always the trust policy, so check <code>sub</code>/<code>aud</code> first.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a project story, which part do interviewers value most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"How long it took","ok":false},{"t":"Which tools you used","ok":false},{"t":"What you learned and now do differently","ok":true},{"t":"Who caused the problem","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>lesson</strong> shows judgement and growth, and stops the story being just a list of facts.</p>","topic":"Interview","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m275--type-html-html-in-a-project-s"}}]},{"title":"The roadmap: what you'd improve","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"Ordered by impact","blocks":[{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[\"1. Data tier<br/>RDS Postgres Multi-AZ<br/>(or EFS)\"] --> B[\"2. HA<br/>desired_count ≥ 2<br/>+ autoscaling\"]\n  B --> C[\"3. Safety<br/>circuit breaker rollback<br/>TG → /healthz\"]\n  C --> D[\"4. Least privilege<br/>scoped CI role<br/>sub → main / env\"]\n  D --> E[\"5. Delivery<br/>plan on PR<br/>prod approval\"]\n  E --> F[\"6. Ops<br/>alarms · dashboards<br/>ECR in bootstrap\"]"}]},{"type":"card","title":"How to answer \"what would you improve?\"","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Pick <strong>one</strong>, explain <strong>why first</strong>, and say <strong>how</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“First I'd move the data out of the container. Memos supports Postgres, so I'd add RDS in Multi-AZ, pass the DSN from Secrets Manager through the task definition, and allow 5432 only from the ECS security group. That makes the tasks stateless, which unlocks running two or more across both AZs.”</p>\n</blockquote>"}]}]},{"title":"Handling hard questions","cards":[{"type":"card","title":"When you don't know","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Don't bluff. Bridge from what you <strong>do</strong> know:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“I haven't used Datadog hands-on yet, but I understand it's an observability platform for metrics, logs, traces and APM. I've used CloudWatch Logs and metrics for Memos, so the troubleshooting principles carry over, and I'd expect to pick it up quickly.”</p>\n</blockquote>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Think out loud","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>For scenario questions:\n1. <strong>Clarify:</strong> \"Is this production? Did anything change recently?\"\n2. <strong>Structure:</strong> \"I'd work outside in: DNS, load balancer, targets, the task, then the logs.\"\n3. <strong>Prioritise:</strong> \"If users are affected, I'd roll back first.\"\n4. <strong>Close:</strong> \"Then I'd add an alarm so we catch it sooner next time.\"</p>"}]},{"type":"card","title":"Questions to ask them","blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"What does the deployment pipeline look like today, and what would you most like to improve?\"</li>\n<li>\"How do you handle on-call and post-mortems?\"</li>\n<li>\"What would success look like in my first six months?\"</li>\n<li>\"Which skills are essential from day one, and which would I be supported to grow?\"</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"type":"check","title":"Quick check","mcq":{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An interviewer asks about a tool you've never used. What's the best approach?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Pretend you've used it","ok":false},{"t":"Say \"I don't know\" and stop","ok":false},{"t":"Be honest, explain what you understand it does, and link it to similar tools you have used","ok":true},{"t":"Change the subject to Memos","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Honesty plus a bridge to related experience shows you can learn, which is what they're really testing.</p>","topic":"Interview","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m276--type-html-html-an-interviewer"}}]}]}];
